{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd","id":1499154,"title":"Homebrew vehicular and combined arms combat in CofD","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-12-08T11:52:20-08:00","id":1499154,"title":"Homebrew vehicular and combined arms combat in CofD","content":"Howdy there,<br /> <br /> I'm coming to <i>Chronicles of Darkness</i> from a background in <i>Dark Heresy</i>, <i>GURPS High Tech 4th Edition</i> and <i>Phoenix Command Mechanised Combat System</i>. I've been recently working on modernising and adding my own detailed rules to both <i>GURPS</i> and <i>Phoenix Command Mechanised Combat System</i> for military vehicles, since PCMCS has been out of print since the 1990's. Work such as data and research is readily transferrable from one system to another, so I've been working on porting some of the rules and content that I make in those systems over to the <i>Chronicles of Darkness</i> Storyteller System.<br /> <br /> I originally started this for my own needs and as a creative exercise, but I'm curious to know if anyone would be interested in using any of this since if there's any demand, I could try to polish it up and put it on Storyteller's Vault as a pay-what-you want sort of affair if anyone feels like they'd get any use out of it, or if anyone has a burning need for armoured vehicles and vehicle mounted weapons right now I could simply show you my rough draft. Or if there are any treadheads here that would like to work on something like it together, I'm open to it.<br /> <br /> I derived algorithms based off the damage figures in the books based on the examples included for the weapons and their real-life characteristics, and used those to infer a damage model. The damage model I scaled relative to the book, by extrapolating some figures based off the Core Rulebook and from Armoury 1st Edition. Using a very strictly &quot;by-the-numbers&quot; approach helps to reduce the risk that I might start unintentionally playing favourites and help to keep the tables objective and I can explain more about how I did it.<br /> <br /> The rough draft I have is in varying stages of completeness, but I have a few choice excerpts that I'm happy to post if anyone's curious. I have a couple of tanks from different eras already finished, including one from the First World War, then a few choice examples from the Second World War, the early to late Cold War, and the present, and I've also started on other content such as 17th Century Naval Artillery and have detailed mechanics on blackpowder guns, since my players play <i>Changeling: The Lost</i> and plan to do something pirate-themed in the far future.<br /> <br /> I'll follow up in a bit with some choice excerpts and explain some of my design decisions and my design process. I'm calling my creation <i>&quot;Gods of Iron&quot;</i> although I developed it under the working title of <i>&quot;Warzone.&quot;</i> At this point in time:<br /> <br /> Part 4, Ancient Weapons, is on Page 67.<br /> Part 5, Modern Small Arms, is on Page 87.<br /> Part 6, Tactical and Heavy Weaponry, is on Page 117. Breechloading cannons for tanks and other AFVs, are on Page 142.<br /> Part 7, WMDs and nuclear weapons are on Page 169.<br /> Part 8, Vehicles, are on Page 173. Currently, only tanks are filled out.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><u><b>Permalink To The Content</b></u></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><b><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1B6ZMR4u9NUB1p_wS6aksK-qaTxabgF4D/view?usp=share_link\" target=\"_blank\">Click here for the Google Drive link</a></b></span><br /> <br /> It's too long to post here, since it's more than 230 pages.<br /> <br /> Please note that it's very work in progress at this point and some stuff was cross referenced from 1st Edition, before I got Hurt Locker. I'm slowly overhauling those portions to align with Hurt Locker: specifically, explosions in Gods of Iron work slightly differently, but require a bit more math to work out. These rules can be substituted with Hurt Locker's, with the exception of nuclear weapons, which use their own mechanics.<br /> <br /> <b>Please understand this is a rough draft.</b> Some sections are missing or placeholders for future content, the formatting is temporary and there is no fancy decoration nor illustrations, and there may be mistakes or typographical issues. Some weapon stats, namely for the 30mm cannons are temporary, or some balance issues or some temporary values in here that are left over, or some values that may make no sense. I will be working on these as I go to continuously improve the draft, but this represents a snapshot of my current progress.<br /> <br /> Any interest or feedback that may help me make a better document is greatly appreciated.<br /> <br /> I'm going to include some of my thoughts below in follow-up posts, which should explain some of my design process, which isn't yet in the draft.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><b>Update Log</b></span><br /> Due to spam detection I will no longer be editing this post, but I will instead be putting it in follow-ups.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My Project to convert Chronicles of Darkness to a data-driven <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd\" target=\"_blank\">Combined Arms Simulation: Gods of Iron</a><br /> My personal website, projects and games: <a href=\"https://puckerfactorgames.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://puckerfactorgames.com</a>","edited":{"userid":27707,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","user":"Jester-PFG","date":". 07-31-2023, 12:40 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","name":"Jester-PFG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27707_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-08T11:55:46-08:00","id":1499155,"title":"","content":"Before I get to my thought processes, I'd like to give a heads-up that it's going to get a wee bit nerdy in here.<br /> <br /> <b><u><span style=\"font-size:16px\">Design rationale</span></u></b><br /> <br /> The long and short of this, I wanted to make some advanced rules for vehicular combat in Chronicles of Darkness and have deep and nuanced mechanics for tanks and AFVs. As a part of my &quot;guiding vision&quot; I considered the following:<ul><li>The advanced rules would build upon, but not replace the existing rules if I could: no replacing the wounding system. It would be best to continue using the structure, durability, and size scores where possible.</li> <li>I would be trying to go for accuracy and maintaining &quot;tactical correctness&quot; rather than going for &quot;rule of cool&quot; or what would provide the most dramatic uncertainty.</li> <li>With a few exceptions, such as the use of nuclear weapons, the intersection of the supernatural with armoured fighting vehicles (such as use of Contracts such as Changeling Hours in Changeling: The Lost or &quot;The Marionette&quot; in Geist: The Sin Eaters) I would consider out of scope. I felt that this should be left up to each specific table to determine, as this can greatly influence the &quot;genre&quot; of their game.</li> <li>To reduce the likelihood of favouritism, I would opt for a data-driven approach, and work on creating algorithms or procedures to transcribe real-world data to in-game units.</li> <li>Game balance would be achieved by ensuring vehicles of comparable era, purpose, role, and capability are used on opposing forces, rather than trying to nerf or buff. I call this philosophy &quot;apples to apples balance&quot; or the use of like with like.</li> </ul><br /> <br /> <br /> <b><u><span style=\"font-size:16px\">Establishing a baseline</span></u></b><br /> <br /> The first thing I needed to do was establish a baseline, because vehicle armour value would drive damage values. Armour penetration is an extremely niche science, and you could write several Ph.D theses on it alone, and many of the precise details of the projectiles are classified. So to make it simpler to convert real world metrics to the game, I decided it would be best to use Rolled Homogenous Armour equivalency which is a common benchmark used in the real world. Vehicle armour could be considered the equivalent of cover, using the rules for damage to it (ignore all weapons that don't have a damage rating equal to or greater than its Durability) [Chronicles of Darkness, Core Rulebook, P.91].<br /> <br /> I decided to start with an .30-06 M2 Armour Piercing Round (MIL-C-1617A), which is publicly available. The document for MIL-C-1617A states that the M2AP round must average at least 0.42&quot; deep into a 7/8&quot; rolled homogenous armour (MIL-A-12560) plate at 100 yards. In practice, an M2AP round can sometimes punch through and sometimes be stopped by a 13mmRHA plate. Chronicles of Darkness states that a .30-06 rifle is Damage 4, AP0 [Chronicles of Darkness, Core Rulebook, P.268]. Armoury 1st Edition is incompatible with 2nd, but suggested a value of AP3 for weapons of its calibre when firing &quot;Armour Piercing&quot; ammunition [Armoury 1st Edition, P.82]. From this, we can determine that a .30-06 M2 Armour Piercing Round would be in the realm of 4AP2 or 4AP3.<br /> <br /> By comparison a 5.56x45mm NATO M855 (SS109) Penetrator Round, while not considered armour piercing, can reliably punch through a 1/4&quot; thick structural-grade A-36 steel plate placed vertically at 100m. Chronicles of Darkness suggests that Durability 3 represent objects made of steel or iron [Chronicles of Darkness, Core Rulebook, P.96]. 2nd Edition suggests that a 5.56x45mm Assault Rifle is Damage 3 AP 0 [Chronicles of Darkness, Core Rulebook, P.268].<br /> <br /> So I decided to approximate things:<ul><li>Mild or structural steel plate (A-36 Structural Steel) could be thought of as being the equivalent of one quarter of the protective value of high-strength rolled homogenous steel plate (MIL-A-12560), but I rounded it up to half to make the math simpler since human beings are good at determining half. If greater resolution is desired, it could be reduced to the original value and different grades, such as high strength steel, high hardness steel, face-hardened alloy steel, or high-strength steels used in automotive applications (such as truck frames) could be represented by changing the coefficient.</li> <li>The chart of vehicle armour would start at 10mm, with Durability 7.</li> <li>Because I wanted things to scale easily and make the math relatively simple, I decided to use a simple linear function to estimate them. Normally, I'd use linear interpolation but I figured what I could do would be to measure it in centimetres, multiply by one and a half, and add the minimum-1, then simply discard all fractions without rounding. This forms our baseline.</li> </ul><br /> I chose doing this because after rounding off, this allowed the amounts of Durability to mmRHA to scale by alternately 1 and then 2 for each centimetre of thickness, which then works out to Durability 7 for the first centimetre, then 3 for each two centimetres beyond it. This allowed me to establish a 3cm thick ballistic grade steel plate as Durability 10, a 50cm thick ballistic grade steel plate will be durability 13, and so on and so on. I did this to scale linearly, and make it easy for someone to do it mentally, without needing a calculator. Again, there are more sophisticated ways of doing this but I chose the path of least resistance.<br /> <br /> These estimates assume thickness relative to the surface normal. For line-of-sight thickness, simply repeat the operation for line-of-sight thickness. I've included the formula in my draft.<br /> <br /> For measuring armour scores, I would use the largest non-inert values on the intended striking face.<br /> <br /> Although no vehicles today use rolled homogenous armour primarily., it's still a common benchmark in explaining how difficult it is to penetrate the armour, even though nowadays we use spaced armour or composite armour. I added some simple &quot;rules of thumb&quot; to allow spaced or composite armour to be approximated based on their characteristics, which I've explained under <i>Armour Types</i>.<br /> <br /> Edit on December 9th: Earlier I stated that the M2 AP round would punch through a metal plate. This has been clarified to state that while it can, it's not a guarantee. I also made a small mistake stating that no vehicles today use mmRHAe, what I meant instead was that no vehicles <i>primarily rely on</i> RHA alone. This has been fixed","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My Project to convert Chronicles of Darkness to a data-driven <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd\" target=\"_blank\">Combined Arms Simulation: Gods of Iron</a><br /> My personal website, projects and games: <a href=\"https://puckerfactorgames.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://puckerfactorgames.com</a>","edited":{"userid":27707,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","user":"Jester-PFG","date":".  Factual mistake. 12-09-2022, 10:22 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","name":"Jester-PFG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27707_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-08T12:08:30-08:00","id":1499156,"title":"","content":"<b><u><span style=\"font-size:16px\">Vehicular scale weapons and damage</span></u></b><br /> <br /> Now that we have a baseline figured out, let's figure out how damage interacts with armour. In real life, armour and weapons have a relationship not at all unlike a pair of training partners: they grow with opposition. Throughout history, weapons have improved and forced armour or designs for survivability to improve, which then forces weapons to improve in a distributed, worldwide game of leap-frog.<br /> <br /> The goal for this is simple, and works well with the existing cover mechanics: to avoid tracking large amounts of health (and needing to decide on health tracks), what I'm going to be doing is instead considering vehicle armour as cover for the squishy and vulnerable people on the inside, along with components such as the engine, the turret drive components and the gun breech, the fuel tanks and the ammunition stores their own objects, each with their own Durability, Size and Structure scores. This keeps the amount of math we're going to have to do down and makes it easier to stat up more vehicles in the future.<br /> <br /> Western testing measures armour penetration by gauging the thickness of the armour plate, with the armour plate angled at 30 degrees and placed at set intervals away from the gun. In order to be considered a success, the rounds need to be able to penetrate the armour 50% of the time.<br /> <br /> This gives us a couple ways to work our way forward. What we can do is we can convert the surface normal to line-of-sight thickness with a bit of basic trignometry if we have to. Although Chronicles is a Success system, we are using d10s which makes things easier and it's easier to simply use the number shown on the dice rather than roll entire handfuls of dice and count them up. From there, we can bear in mind that the average roll of 1d10 is 6, and work our way backwards.<br /> <br /> For example, if we wanted to show a weapon that could penetrate 150mm of armour plate, placed vertically, by Western standards, we derive the thickness of the 150mm thick armour plate at 0 degrees by the steps above, which gives us Durability 28. A weapon that could penetrate it at &lt;100m would need to roll a total (in terms of damage plus armour piercing) of 28 or more half the time. We can get the number by subtracting 6 from this total, which gives us 22. Therefore, the combined amount would need to be 1d10+22.<br /> <br /> Of course, there's a perfectly good armour penetration mechanic in Chronicles already which we can take advantage of. I decided to do the same thing as I did above for armour and do the same for weapons, and create standardized bore sizes for different weapons, with different weapons resolving to differest sizes, and then the difference made up by the armour piercing value. This chart only is intended to cover medium to high velocity projectiles, so I needed to estimate them, since the game system itself, even Hurt Locker, leaves off at &quot;Elephant Gun&quot; as a 5L weapon. Although the diameter of the bullet is smaller, a .500 Nitro Express or even a .600 Nitro Express produces significantly less muzzle velocity and therefore less energy than a 12.7x99mm NATO (.50 BMG) M2 Ball round (~11.4kJ vs ~18kJ at both rounds' maximum potential, respectively). So I had to build off that. Please understand that this will countermand some of the tables in Armoury 1st Edition and Hurt Locker, but it is being done for the reason of realism, not for dramatic conservation.<br /> <br /> To plot the damage characteristics of different bore diameters, I plotted some lines experimentally, with points for 12.7, 14.5, 20, 25, 30, 40, 50, 60, 75, 88, 90, 120, 125, 127, 155 and 406mm shells (I want to add battleships to my work at a future date, so I went as far as including a 16&quot; shell), and tried to get them to align by process of trial and error. This involved some guesswork, but I decided to scale a 20mm shell relative to a 14.5mm shell. This would make a 20mm shell damage 8, a 25mm to be damage 9, and a 30mm to be damage 10, then continue from there via linear interpolation. This should represent the median damage that a shell should do assuming zero AP value for a medium to high velocity gun.<br /> <br /> From there, we can then make up the difference using the Armour Penetration values. So for instance, let us say that there is a hypothetical 75mm cannon shell that has been established via testing in the wartime to penetrate 160mm of steel plate angled at 0 degrees, at a distance of 100m that we want to transcribe into Chronicles of Darkness. 75mm works out from the above to be an average of 19. 1d10+14 will place the median value at 19.<br /> <br /> I would like to use mechanics for constraining minimums and maximums, however, I don't have any set mechanics for that and simply rely on 1d10 for 75mm and above, which allows performance to vary by about a third in either direction. For now I've tried to follow suit as much as I can in this manner; smaller weapons may use 1d5, or roll to score successes. Larger weapons, such as 155mm Artillery, or 5&quot; Naval Guns may roll 2d10, or even 3d10 in the case of battleship guns. Multiple dice getting involved tends to change the distribution, since the more dice, the steeper the distribution gets, so this'll have to be something to evaluate further in the future; a good compromise is to use one standard deviation above or below as a margin for distributions that aren't linear, which accounts for roughly 68% of all results.<ul><li>We look up the chart of bore diameters of medium to high velocity guns and see that for a 75mm gun, the damage base is 1d10+14.</li> <li>We look at the measurement for 160mm of armour and see it is Durability 30.</li> <li>Since the average roll of 1d10 is 6, and the damage base of 14 will only sum to 20, and that leaves us with a deficit of 10, we add the deficit via the Armour Piercing value.</li> <li>The damage of this hypothetical shell is then raised to 1d10+14 AP10.</li> <li>This means that 50% of the time, the damage characteristic of the weapon will be equal to 30. Since successes on the attack roll do not contribute to damaging cover, this will be enough to be equal to and overcome the 160mm of ballistic plate 50% of the time, mirroring the odds of successful penetration at a distance of 100m at that given range.</li> <li>If DDO is factored in (in the next post) we readjust until DDO is taken into account.</li> </ul><br /> Should the standardized rating be higher than the amount, what we can do is either the following:<ul><li>I chose to add a new weapon characteristic, called &quot;Low Velocity&quot; which would count the Durability of modern armour as being double its normal durability, and then recalculate from there. This would allow me to represent older or ancient ammunition types, such as blackpowder cannonballs (because I've wanted to do 17th century artillery and cannons and muskets later on). If need be, it's possible to build on that for &quot;Super Low Velocity&quot; and &quot;Extremely Low Velocity&quot; which would quadruple, and then octuple the armour value respectively.</li> <li>Reduce the amount of damage done to make up the extra.</li> </ul><br /> Both options could be used depending on circumstance and context.<br /> <br /> Note that in most of his books, R.P. Hunnicutt gives estimation for penetration taken at 1000 yards and 2000 yards, with homogeneous armour at 30 degrees obliquity [R.P. Hunnicut: Firepower - A History of the American Heavy Tank, P.211; Sherman - A History of the American Medium Tank, P.558]. We may be able to work our way backwards from these. Note that obliquity has greater implications than simply line of sight thickness, especially given that it takes advantage of older full-bore armour piercing shells' tendency to ricochet. However, this is a massive rabbithole that would start becoming complicated in a hurry, so the increased likelihood of causing ricochets on top of the increase of line-of-sight thickness is being ignored.<br /> <br /> Although this isn't as reliable as Hunnicutt's research, it's possible to use War Thunder's data in interim, because it's neatly formatted, readily available, and is arranged at intervals that closely align rather than simply being at 1000m and 2000m. For now, that's what I've gone with for some of the values, in the interest of expediency -- more on that below, under &quot;DDO&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My Project to convert Chronicles of Darkness to a data-driven <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd\" target=\"_blank\">Combined Arms Simulation: Gods of Iron</a><br /> My personal website, projects and games: <a href=\"https://puckerfactorgames.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://puckerfactorgames.com</a>","edited":{"userid":27707,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","user":"Jester-PFG","date":".  Minor fixup 12-09-2022, 10:28 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","name":"Jester-PFG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27707_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-08T12:29:24-08:00","id":1499157,"title":"","content":"<b><u><span style=\"font-size:18px\">Damage Drop Off</span></u></b><br /> <br /> I added some extra rules for how air resistance makes rounds less effective as range increases. Again, this is intended to mirror real-life odds since distance is an important tactical consideration. Damage Drop Off is measured as slash-separated numbers, treating the air as cover at short, medium, long ranges and beyond.<br /> <br /> An important consideration is that air resistance plays an important part in tank combat. Historically, a very big advantage of bigger tanks and casemate tank destroyers was not just that they could carry bigger guns, (in the real world, there are no &quot;hit points&quot;) but rather, that their bigger guns retained their effectiveness over greater distances.<br /> <br /> This has important game balance considerations, since simply making heavy tanks inflict more damage and lighter tanks inflict less doesn't necessarily capture the nuance that distance yields important consequences for different tanks, especially given that we've gone the distance to accurately mirror penetration. The same logic of simply reducing damage done by the lighter guns also doesn't capture the nuance that closer ranges tended to make up for lower muzzle velocities when it came to the odds of successfully punching through armour plate. The good news is that Chronicles of Darkness already has existing range brackets for short, medium, and long range (which can easily be extended to extreme ranges and beyond), so let's use this. I call this rule &quot;DDO&quot; for &quot;Damage Drop Off&quot;<br /> <br /> The easiest way to do this is to recalculate damage for different ranges, and then change the deficit by penalising armour penetration first, then damage once armour penetration reaches zero, to determine how damage &quot;falls off&quot; for kinetic projectiles.<br /> <br /> So let's return to our theoretical 75mm hypothetical cannon that penetrates 160mm of armour at 100m. Let's say that it has a range of 1000m/2000m/4000m, which, respectively, real world testing either provides as 140mmRHAe, 100mmRHAe, 80mmRHAe. First, let's slap an extra close-range bracket on it we'll call point blank, representing half of short range, to turn the ranges to PB/1000m/2000m/4000m, and recalculate the difference at each one. This gives us 0/-3/-7/-12, indicating that the gun will do:<br /> <br /> 1d10+14 AP10 at 0m to 500m,<br /> 1d10+14 AP7 at 501m to 1000m,<br /> 1d10+14 AP3 at 1001m to 2000m,<br /> 1d10+10 AP0 beyond 2000m.<br /> <br /> We can continue to add or estimate more values beyond 4000m if need be by estimating DDO (easy way, just look at the difference between DDO, as many cannons could be fired indirectly as artillery. I chose to make it this way since the amount of penetration lost to aerodynamics is nonlinear. This represents a fairly simple way of doing it because only two numbers need to be added at a given range and all of it can be added up in the same roll to damage.<br /> <br /> Thus, this establishes that distance for kinetic projectiles plays a significant role: survivability increases with distance, which increases the effectiveness of armour or decreases the effectiveness of kinetic armour piercing rounds such as AP rounds. HEAT, HE, HESH, and the other types of rounds which depend on stored chemical energy are not subject to this limitation.<br /> <br /> Mechanics for thermobaric/fuel air explosives and different types of bombs are due later. For HEAT rounds, I reversed the order of precedence when resolving them: damage first, explosive force after, with HEAT rounds never having explosive force damage cumulative with the direct damage they inflict on a single target.<br /> <br /> The blast radius of the US Navy Mark 7 16&quot;/50-calibre Naval Guns' Mark 13 High Capacity Shells were estimated based on historical records of the Mk. 13 HC shells blowing down all the trees in a 400m radius when conducting shore bombardment during the Vietnam War, and were scaled according to the rules in the books. The rest of the explosive radiuses of the shells in my current draft are currently temporary and expected to change. They should not be considered final.<br /> <br /> <b><u><span style=\"font-size:18px\">Ammunition Types</span></u></b><br /> <br /> Ammunition types can at this moment be determined by using Hurt Locker as a bit of a baseline. At this moment I'm working on mechanics that would allow us to calculate blast radius, Damage and Force dice from charge and fragmentation mass.<br /> <br /> In interim, I'll be working my baseline off some very rough estimates based on the scaling and relative masses of grenades from Hurt Locker, and a very approximate blast radius of HELLFIRE missiles, 155mm Artillery Shells, and a 2000-pound munition, with estimates taken from a very simplified handout from the UN Human Rights Office of the High Commissioner [<a href=\"https://www.ohchr.org/sites/default/files/Documents/HRBodies/HRCouncil/CoIGaza/Kill_Radius_Compared.pdf\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.ohchr.org/sites/default/..._Compared.pdf</a>]. The Geneva International Centre for Humanitarian Demining has some information on case studies of casualties and the nature of injuries caused by mortar shells, which can have shells of explosive capacity ranging from the size of a few ounces of TNT to more than 4kg of explosive filler [<a href=\"http://characterisationexplosiveweapons.org/studies/annex-c-81-82-120-mm-mortars/\" target=\"_blank\">http://characterisationexplosiveweap...0-mm-mortars/</a>].<br /> <br /> This is going to be really work in progress so I'll be continuing with this in what time I can get. Expect heavy changes to happen here as I go and tune the models.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My Project to convert Chronicles of Darkness to a data-driven <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd\" target=\"_blank\">Combined Arms Simulation: Gods of Iron</a><br /> My personal website, projects and games: <a href=\"https://puckerfactorgames.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://puckerfactorgames.com</a>","edited":{"userid":27707,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","user":"Jester-PFG","date":".  Repair broken links 12-09-2022, 10:37 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","name":"Jester-PFG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27707_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-08T13:58:15-08:00","id":1499168,"title":"","content":"<b><u><span style=\"font-size:16px\">Acceleration, Handling, and More</span></u></b><br /> <br /> I decided to next take a look at acceleration. Generally, Chronicles of Darkness assumes that most vehicles may accelerate at a rate of 5m per turn, but or 10m per turn should the driver succeed at a Dexterity + Drive roll, penalised by the vehicle's handling modifier. Fast vehicles may accelerate at 10, and slow vehicles may only accelerate at 5m per turn regardless of the driver's skill.<br /> <br /> Because this doesn't capture some of the granualarity of what I'd like to accomplish, what I've done instead is assume that each turn is 3 seconds long. This allows me to then determine acceleration by determining the vehicle's 0-60 time (if applicable) or the time it takes for a vehicle to reach its maximum speed, then averaging and converting the acceleration to a 3-second interval in metres per turn per turn.<br /> <br /> Turn radius, I'm still working on. Formula One cars can usually turn at rates where the G forces incurred by the turn routinely get to multiple times the force of gravity at speeds well in excess of 350km/h (this is why Formula One cars often need dry-sump lubrication to prevent oil starvation), which establishes a pretty good upper limit, however we'll work our way backwards at a later date.<br /> <br /> An easy way to do it is in interim is to do it GURPS style, with radius being equal to speed squared plus an adjustment factor. For tanks, the limiting on a tank's turning radius tend to be the tracks: too aggressive a turn, and the tracks can be &quot;thrown.&quot; For now, some values have been placed in interim.<br /> <br /> <b><u><span style=\"font-size:16px\">Turret Traverse Speed (TTS)</span></u></b><br /> <br /> Because it can take time for tank gun turrets to traverse, I've also added an extra mechanic that dictates how many turns it takes for a tank turret to rotate through 90 degrees, and how many turns it takes for a tank gun to &quot;identify&quot; (or point) at a target. This I've expressed as two numbers or figures separated by a slash<i>. </i><br /> <br /> Some WWII tanks, such as the Panzer IV Ausf. J did not have electric drive for the turret traverse, because of late-war era shortages which forced the electric drive to be omitted: as a result they can take as much as 11 turns (representing 33 seconds of in-game time) to turn the turret 90 degrees, less depending on the gunner's Stamina. I've recorded this on Page 197 as of this revision.<br /> <br /> This is an important balance consideration since it and the Strength of the loader bottlenecks how quickly a tank gun can be readied to fire, and also affects what direction the tank gun can be fired in relative to the hull facing at that point in time.<br /> <br /> <b><u><span style=\"font-size:16px\">Accuracy and Target Differential Divisor (Acc and TDD)</span></u></b><br /> <br /> During the Second World War, it wasn't impossible for tanks and tank guns to slug it out very long distances: a Nashorn armed with an 8.8cm L/71 PaK 43 claims a kill from 2,800m against a Sherman [Marko Pantelic, <a href=\"https://tanks-encyclopedia.com/ww2/nazi_germany/sd-kfz-164_nashorn.php\" target=\"_blank\">https://tanks-encyclopedia.com/ww2/n...4_nashorn.php</a>]. The Hull of an M4A3 Sherman is 118&quot; wide without its mud guards, or around 3m wide. To be able to land a hit within a circle inscribed within its width at that distance would require accuracy on the order of 1-2 minute of arc, which is approximately the accuracy of a good quality sniper rifle (in order to be considered &quot;Match Grade&quot; a rifle firing match grade ammunition must be capable of accuracy under one minute of arc).<br /> <br /> Keep in mind that everything's relative: a +/-0.001&quot; of variation on a 1&quot; diameter shaft, is actually the same amount of tolerance as +/-0.1&quot; of varation on a 100&quot; diameter shaft. Given the size difference between the 8.8cm gun and a modern 7.62x51mm rifle, this indicates that the grades of tolerance on the 8.8cm gun are actually going to need to be much tighter than a match grade rifle!<br /> <br /> The magnification of the tank's optics are also going to be as highly sophisticated, if not more complex than a rifle's of the equivalent era and offer greater magnification. So all that said, tank guns are very accurate, so simply slapping on a minimum size characteristic seems a little arbitrary, yet, there's a certain amount of size that these guns are designed to be turned on.<br /> <br /> For this reason, I've developed a mechanic I call Accuracy and Target Difference Divisor. Accuracy representing the minimum size of a target that the gun can fire on without penalty, and TDD, which then divides the difference. These are intended to vary by range, so as to offer more realistic way of determining what targets are and aren't appropriate for a tank gun.<br /> <br /> For example: a Tank Gun has a range of PB/1000/2000/4000, and has an Accuracy of 5/5/7/10, and a TDD of 1/1/1/2. This means that at point blank and short range, it may shoot at targets size 5 and over without penalty. However, at medium range, targets have a minimum size of 7, and take a -1 penalty for the difference if less. At long range, targets must be size 10 or take a -1 penalty for every 2 of the difference, rounding up.<br /> <br /> So a tank gun firing at a target size 5 would take no penalties at point blank or short range, a -2 penalty at medium range, then -3 at long range, on top of the distance penalties of -1 and -2 respectively, but not inflict any penalty when shooting at a size 10 target.<br /> <br /> I intend to use this to allow realistic targeting of tanks as a unit, or different components on a tank, such as the turret, or hull, or components such as the engine compartment or ammunition racks: in video games like War Thunder that's possible and expected because War Thunder makes it significantly easier, in real life these hits happened much more through luck and chance than by design.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b><u><span style=\"font-size:16px\">Scattering and Circular Error Probable</span></u></b><br /> <br /> Chronicles of Darkness is difficult to compute scattering for, since normally with systems that use the number shown on the dice and require it to be greater than or less than a set target number, it's easy to determine how much something failed by. With the Storyteller system, this isn't so intuitively done, so I'm still working on this - currently the mechanics I have in there are temporary.<br /> <br /> To use a Circular Error Probable mechanic, what I've decided to do is take the circular error probable of a weapon, measured in metres, multiply it by percentile dice, and multiply that by 0.03, then roll directional dice of 1d8, representing north, northeast, east, etc, which for now is &quot;good enough&quot; but I may replace this at a later date. I've included an example, although the damage tables are all off since it was older and used temporary values.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My Project to convert Chronicles of Darkness to a data-driven <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd\" target=\"_blank\">Combined Arms Simulation: Gods of Iron</a><br /> My personal website, projects and games: <a href=\"https://puckerfactorgames.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://puckerfactorgames.com</a>","edited":{"userid":27707,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","user":"Jester-PFG","date":".  Fix broken link 12-09-2022, 10:36 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","name":"Jester-PFG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27707_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-09T17:08:25-08:00","id":1499265,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jester-PFG</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd#post1499154\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Howdy there,<br /> <br /> I'm coming to <i>Chronicles of Darkness</i> from a background in <i>Dark Heresy</i>, <i>GURPS High Tech 4th Edition</i> and <i>Phoenix Command Mechanised Combat System</i>. . . .</div> </div> </div> </div>I've played GURPS once and I don't remember the system and you're right, there are no hit-points.<br /> <br /> I did create a vehicular-combat-system for the original WoD; hey, I wanted to run a World War Two game (Eastern-Front), where players had humanity and had to commit atrocities etc. And a few of my players were pro-German etc. <br /> <br /> My background is<i> Battletech, Cyperpunk: 2020: Maximum-Metal and Steel Panthers</i> (DOS video-game). So, each cannon and vehicle had to have their unique armor and damage. I based a lot of it off the Vehicle-combat system from, Vampire: the Player's Guide. <br /> <br /> At first, I had to use my judgement on how much damage was done; does an AP-round kill the entire crew and I apply remaining damage to all or just a few of the crew? Surely and HE-round with an area-of-effect would damage all the crew etc. I made a random-chart, which was a lot easier because I didn't have to decide.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"740"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-09T18:54:56-08:00","id":1499266,"title":"","content":"Hello there Iguazu, I appreciate the interest! That's awesome to see that more people are interested in vehicle and vehicle combat, I'll definitely will need to take a look at the Vampire Player Guide's vehicle combat system, I very much like seeing how other people have approached the problem before.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> At first, I had to use my judgement on how much damage was done; does an AP-round kill the entire crew and I apply remaining damage to all or just a few of the crew? Surely and HE-round with an area-of-effect would damage all the crew etc. I made a random-chart, which was a lot easier because I didn't have to decide. </div> </div> </div>Sure, I'd be more than happy to break this down a tiny bit for you with two examples of how I've handled it in my version. It's a little bit more complex, but I felt that it offered a little extra to give back in that it allowed for finer control in situations.<br /> <br /> Gods of Iron uses a &quot;module by module&quot; system. Notice that each tank in the PDF has its own Durability and Structures chart, which includes the Turret and the different facings for it, the Hull and its different facings, but also Systems, which include the engine, ammunition hold, fuel tanks, and the gun breech (which are used to represent the turret traverse and elevation mechanisms as well as the gun). Most importantly, each individual crewmember is considered a module, so damage to crews is also something that I want to capture.<br /> <br /> The way I've written it at this point in time is that whenever a weapon that has no bursting charge doing at least Damage 8 or more strikes something with Durability 3 or greater, it creates a spall, from causing pieces of itself and the armour to shatter, sending razor sharp shards of metal whizzing around the interior of the tank.<br /> <br /> This spall is represented by doing damage dice equal to 1/4 of the damage characteristic of the weapon, and its blast radius is equal to a third of the weapon's damage, and depending on whether or not the damage has the &quot;LV&quot; (Low Velocity) modifier or not, this influences whether or not the damage dice also inherit the Low Velocity trait. (&quot;Low Velocity&quot; simply doubles the armour values, unless they are non-ballistic). Spalls are limited by the Durability of the armour they hit, which I did intentionally so that in some cases, armour piercing tank shells that have a penetration fuse can pass through lightly armoured vehicles such as civilian vehicles without causing much damage, just as they would in the real world.<br /> <br /> Depending on the presence of a Penetrator with Enhanced Lateral Effects (PELE), this is done a bit differently, but in this case, I'll pick an example from WWII:<br /> <br /> The vehicle takes the initial damage, and then depending on where it's hit, the blast affects other areas in the tank. If an area is targeted, the shell hits the targeted module after resolving cover. In the future, I want to have &quot;Confined Space&quot; mechanics to magnify the effect of a blast that's been contained but that will have to be in the future.<br /> <br /> I'll take you through an example of the gameplay how it would be played out at my table.<br /> <br /> Let us take an example of a Sherman Firefly [See the attached PDF, Page 193 to 195, as of this update] armed with the QF-17 Quick Firing 17-Pounder Gun [See the attached PDF, page 145 as of this update], firing the Mark 8 Armour-Piercing Capped, Ballistic Capped shell [See the attached PDF, Page 146] against a Panzer IV Ausf G [See the attached PDF, Page 195]. We are going to say that the Panzer IV and Firefly are facing each other dead-on, at a distance of 1341m.<br /> <br /> Edited to add: To my knowledge the Mk. 8 APCBC shell did not have a bursting charge.<br /> <br /> <b>Example: an untargeted attack</b><br /> <br /> On the Firefly's turn, assuming the gun is loaded and the turret is on target, the gunner elects that he will shoot at the Panzer IV. When the GM asks &quot;Which location?&quot; the gunner elects to shoot at the hull, since the hull is a size 16 target, and at that range Acc and TDD penalties don't apply to size 16 targets, but because the turret is a size 5 target, Acc and TDD penalties at that range would apply. One turn is taken to position the gun onto the target, after which the gunner may aim to acquire bonuses. We'll say that he aims for three turns, collects whatever modifiers he wants, and fires with an attack pool of 8 dice, and gets 7, 6, 3, 9, 8, 1, 3, 4, for a total of two successes.<br /> <br /> The Mark 8 APCBC shell does 1d10+14 AP13 damage, versus the Panzer IV's front glacis of 80mm, which is Durability 17. However, DDO of -8 applies, which reduces the damage to 1d10+14 AP5. The player rolls and gets an 8, which comes out to 22AP5 damage, versus the Panzer IV's front glacis of Durability 17, which has been amended to 12 due to the AP value. From there, this means that 12 damage, plus 2 successes are taken out on the Panzer IV's hull, reducing it from Structure 29 to Structure 15.<br /> <br /> Now, because the shell penetrated, but it wasn't targeted to someone in particular, we can assume that it manages to miraculously navigate its way through the cramped interior of the tank, and possibly comes to a stop. However, the danger is not yet done. Because the QF-17 pounder counted for 22 during that turn, what we do is we use the spalling formula on Page 134: this is done by dividing the damage by 4 to determine number of dice for a lethal attack, with a radius equal to a tenth of the weapon's damage, or 2 metres for ground zero, 4 metres for the primary blast radius, and 8 metres for the secondary blast radius. (Please note that in the past, I used to use &quot;primary, secondary, tertiary&quot; before I got Warzone, some areas in the PDF still use this)<br /> <br /> The area within the Panzer IV is small and cramped, and it's safe to say that everything in the hull and turret would be within 2m of where the Mk. 8 APCBC shell struck. As 22/4 discarding all fractions works out to 5, this means that everyone and everything inside the tank is going to get 5 attack dice rolled against them, with damage being lethal. So we roll five attack dice against all the components inside the tank, this includes the driver, the bow gunner, the commander, the loader, and the gunner, the engine, the ammunition in the hull, the fuel tanks, and the gun breech (which is also used to represent the turret ring and gun elevation mechanism).<br /> <br /> The rules for spalling are similar, but distinct from the rules for explosions: I do not use Hurt Locker's &quot;Ground Zero&quot; rule for them at this moment, but I could rework it for that if need be.<br /> <br /> The GM rolls five dice on each one. By the end of the turn, the Panzer IV has lost 14 points of structure from its hull (twelve damage inflicted, plus two successes), and the driver walks away with 3 lethal damage, the bow gunner with 4, the commander with 2, the gunner with 1, the ammunition takes 3 damage, which reduces to 2, the engine takes 3 damage which reduces to 0, and the fuel tanks take 2 damage, which reduces to 1. However, the fuel tanks have been damaged. The GM rolls one die, representing one die for each point of structure lost on the fuel tanks to a weapon with incendiary properties (such as a HEI or API round, or in this case the red hot fragments of shell and armour plate that just splintered away) to see if any of them cause it to ignite, and gets no successes. The tracks, gun breech, and the turret are undamaged, but the damage was primarily resolved on the hull -- this is where the successes are contributed to.<br /> <br /> In this example, the crew are heavily damaged and bleeding, but they're still alive and the tank can still fight, even after being hit by an AP round.<br /> <br /> <b>Example: an attack targeted to the fuel tank</b><br /> <br /> A more interesting approach is a targeted approach. This would be used if the Sherman's gunner elected to use a targeted attack on one of the modules, typically an ammunition rack or a fuel tank.<br /> <br /> Let's take the same setup, but this time we'll assume that the Sherman's gunner had deliberately aimed for the ammunition rack, which incurs a -5 penalty for shooting at an unseen target, but as the fuel tanks are size 8, so there's no penalty for Accuracy and TDD.<br /> <br /> But rather than the shell being rationalised to have punched through the armour and wind its way through the interior, missing the critical components, and then exit after sending a cloud of razor-sharp fragments all through the tank, instead, the 2 successes from his roll would contribute (almost superfluous) damage to the fuel tank rather than the tank's turret, after making it through the armour and the fuel tank's durability stat of 1. Because the armour of the front is 17, and the fuel tank's Durability is 1, but a roll of 1d10+14 AP13 less DDO of -5 came out to 22AP5 damage, it would inflict a total of 9 damage on the fuel tank after durability and armour.<br /> <br /> Should any of the components such as the fuel tanks or the ammunition rack on the tank be destroyed, they do what would be natural after being struck by a red hot shell that just passed through 3&quot; thick armour plating, becoming hot enough to glow in the process. In this case, it's to the fuel tank. The fuel tank explodes into flames, creating the inferno tilt instantly at full intensity. Damage is resolved on the crew and internal components injuring them as per the previous example, with five damage dice rolled against each one individually.<br /> <br /> More importantly however, the fuel tank has been destroyed, spilling four hundred litres of gasoline into a white hot solid shell. This immediately starts a fire that burns with the intensity of a gasoline fire (+2), and is the size of an inferno (+3), inflicting five lethal damage per turn. This stacks with the damage that the crew received from the initial spall. The first turn, the ammunition in the rack, which is size 6, durability 1, structure 7, loses 4 points of structure, and if it received 1 damage during the spall, it's now down to 2 structure points left.<br /> <br /> The ammunition reaches 0 structure the next turn and explodes. I generally resolve this as resolving one hit from the tank's own weaponry against anyone and anything inside the tank when it blew. When it comes to an ammunition explosion, I might embellish this a little bit by saying the tank's turret would be blasted a hundred feet in the air, and rolling 1d8 (for north, northeast, east, etc) and 1d10 for the number of metres to determine where the turret lands.<br /> <br /> <b>Some closing thoughts on those examples...</b><br /> <br /> Because damage to modules is tracked individually, generally it depends on what gets hit, how it gets hit, and any cascading failures, such as a gas tank or an engine or ammunition rack being destroyed, or a crewmember being killed. Most of the time when I've played this with my playtesters, what tends to happen is that the tanks get &quot;knocked out&quot; by all the crew in them getting killed, or their ammunition or fuel tanks being struck and exploding, then taking the tank with them.<br /> <br /> It is a bit of a strange system to wrap one's head around, but the idea is to capture the effect of tanks being vulnerable to their own stores of fuel and ammunition. It's more complex, but allows for more nuance and allows for tank combat to last a little longer.<br /> <br /> It's my hope that this system of resolving damage individually on each target allows for more depth, since I was thinking that each crewmember potentially could be a player character or an NPC, rather than a nameless stat on a wargame roster, and it would be good to model them and their stations individually.<br /> <br /> Please let me know what you think.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Edited to add: </b>I've also included some examples of tank-on-tank combat in <b>Chapter 8, Section 3, Subsection 1: &quot;Tank Examples of Play&quot; </b>which as of this update is on Page 185, showing an exchange of fire between Soviet T-10M and a US M103A2. This includes the use of HEAT rounds and how they work, and how explosive rounds work when they punch through the tank's armour.<br /> <br /> I haven't quite gotten to HESH rounds yet, I'll have to add those soon. HE rounds I treat as just using the blast and force. The HE rounds don't do a very good job of punching through armour, but just like real life the big danger is if they hit the roof or the belly plate of the the hull, or the turret roof. If the damage exceeds the roof's durability, excess damage is carried over into the crew stations underneath it. This can lead to situations like a round striking the turret face and being absorbed by the frontal turret armour, but the driver, being located under a thin hull roof, not being so lucky.<br /> <br /> <b>Further Edited to Add:</b> I just realised I have made a small mistake on the tank examples of play. The M103A2's 120mm Gun M58's M358 APCBC-T shot should not have a bursting charge, as it is a solid shot but is mistakenly played in the examples of play as having a bursting charge. I will fix this in a future update.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My Project to convert Chronicles of Darkness to a data-driven <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd\" target=\"_blank\">Combined Arms Simulation: Gods of Iron</a><br /> My personal website, projects and games: <a href=\"https://puckerfactorgames.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://puckerfactorgames.com</a>","edited":{"userid":27707,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","user":"Jester-PFG","date":".  Clarifications, correct calculation error, fix some grammar issues, and add some addendums. 12-10-2022, 03:48 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","name":"Jester-PFG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27707_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-13T07:18:35-08:00","id":1499517,"title":"","content":"I decided to provide a small preview to what I provide here, on this forum, to save you guys a bit of searching through a 200+ page document to find a single entry.<br /> <br /> Once again, I know this doesn't look like anything you've probably seen in Chronicles of Darkness, because I've added so many extra features to the game on top of this. However, these optional add-on rules are compatible with Chronicles of Darkness and are scaled to the same relative baseline as I've outlined in the second post. The rules for Terrain Rating, Turret Traverse Speed, Gun Stabilisation, Tactical Diameter, and other detailed mechanics that aren't in the core rulebook or armoury are there to make the tanks handle more realistically; these rules are under Vehicle Rules in the document linked in the first post under &quot;Permalink.&quot;<br /> <br /> This is an entry for the T-72A, and I've included the T-72B and T-72B3 obr.2016 as variants, representing different variants of the T-72 from history at 1976, 1988, and 2016, respectively. The T-72B is intended to be the Soviet counterpart to the M60A3(TTS) I've already added to the PDF. Both the T-72B and M60A3(TTS) are intended to be opponents and represent both sides of the Cold War, and are still in use by several countries around the world to this day. The Russian Ground Forces still rely heavily on the T-72B3, as the T-90M and T-90 had a lower production run.<br /> <br /> The 2A46 smoothbore 125mm gun and its ammunition types are under &quot;Modern Breech Loading Cannons&quot; in &quot;Tactical and Heavy Weaponry&quot;, as of this update, on Page 156. I don't have separate entries for the PKT and the NSV machine guns, but at this moment the M60 Machine Gun (which has the PKM, which is a dismounted version of the PKT listed under &quot;Comparable Weapons&quot;) can be used as a stand-in, same with the M2HB-QCB Browning Machine Gun, as the differences between 12.7x99mm NATO and 12.7x108mm Soviet aren't great enough to merit an entirely new statblock except for vanity/history.<br /> <br /> The T-72A's gun stabiliser was only effective to around ~25km/h on relatively level ground [T-72 Main Battle Tank 1974-93, Steven J. Zaloga], and the combustible-cased ammunition in the T-72A is entirely unprotected within its autoloader, which in turn is housed unprotected in the crew compartment. Both these aspects have been mirrored here by reducing the Gun Stabilisation to only -4, and the Durability of the ammunition hold to 0.<br /> <br /> I will add this to the next update and correct the existing T-90/T-90M entry to update to the newer information.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Playable Game Content</u></b><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <br /> <b><u>T-72A - Basic Stats</u></b><br /> <b>Curb Weight:</b> 41,000kg<br /> <b>Powerplant:</b> V-46-6 V-12 Diesel with H24 Supercharger, 780 hp (580kW)<br /> <b>Maximum Speed:</b> 60km/h (50 metres per turn)<br /> <b>Acceleration:</b> 4 metres per turn, per turn<br /> <b>Handling:</b> -3<br /> <b>Terrain Rating:</b> 4(Max)/ 5(Half)/ 6(Quarter)<br /> <b>Tactical Diameter:</b> Neutral<br /> <b>TTS:</b> 2/1<br /> <b>Gun Stabilisation:</b> -4<br /> <b>Crew:</b> 3: Commander, Driver, Gunner<br /> <b>Fuel Capacity:</b> 705L internally, +2\u00d7275L external tanks<br /> <b>Range:</b> 600km (est)<br /> <b>Armament:</b><br /> 1\u00d7 2A46-1 125mm Smoothbore Gun, 44 rounds<br /> 1\u00d7 PKT 7.62\u00d754mmR Machine Gun, Coaxial, 2000 rounds<br /> 1\u00d7 12.7\u00d7108mm NSV Machine Gun, Commander's Hatch, 300 rounds<br /> <br /> <b><u>T-72A - Durability and Structure</u></b><br /> <b>Turret:</b> Size: 6, Structure: 75<br /> Front (Composite, 450mm RHAe): Durability 69<br /> Sides (240mm, sculpted): Durability 42<br /> Rear (40mm): Durability 12<br /> Roof (40mm): Durability 12<br /> <b>Hull:</b> Size: 12, Structure: 52<br /> Glacis (60mm, sloped 68\u00b0) Durability: 15 (normal), 40 (line of sight)<br /> Sides (80mm): Durability 18<br /> Rear (40mm): Durability 12<br /> Roof over engine deck (20mm): Durability 9<br /> Tracks: Size 7, Durability 9, Structure 16<br /> <b>Systems</b><br /> Engine (in hull): Size 7, Durability 3, Structure 10<br /> Ammunition Hold (hull and turret): Size 6, Durability 0, Structure 6<br /> Fuel Tanks (in hull), Size 10, Durability 1, Structure 11<br /> Gun Breech (in turret), Size 5, Durability 10, Structure 15<br /> Autoloader (turret component), Size 3, Durability 1, Structure 4<br /> Autoloader (hull component), Size 3, Durability 1, Structure 4<br /> <br /> Development for the T-72 dates back as far as 1965, with the acceptance of the T-64, developed under the codename of Objekt 432, into the Soviet military. However, while the T-64 looked good &quot;on paper&quot; it made use of many new technologies all at the same time, such as an all new suspension and an autoloader which were costly to produce and sometimes prone to reliability issues, and only two years later, VPK, the Military Industrial Comission of the USSR aimed to replace the T-64 with something that they hoped would cut the cost of the T-64 by half, designated Objekt 172 and codenamed the &quot;Ural&quot; which eventually would become the T-72. In keeping with the USSR's philosophy of keeping the tanks as small and as light as possible to minimize weight, maximize protection, and reduce target silhouette, the T-72 weighs about 41 tonnes.<br /> <br /> By 1968, the first prototype for Objekt 172 was completed. Objekt 172 featured suspension based off the older T-62 for reduced cost and greater reliability. By 1972, the design was completed and production began, which was quickly accepted for Soviet use. Although initially the design was intended only for use within the Soviet Union, by 1976, the Soviet government made the decision to license production of the T-72 to allied countries, including Poland and Czechoslovakia, which produced their own versions.<br /> <br /> The T-72 is so numerous and in service in so many locations, it has a huge number of variants, but at the heart of each T-72 is the 125mm smoothbore 2A46-1 gun mated to a 2Eh28M two-axis stabiliser and a 44-round carousel mounted in the hull. Loading is accomplished by lowering or lifting the gun barrel to 3\u00b0 of elevation, allowing the carousel to rotate the cassette containing the projectile and propellant to the loading position, elevate, and be rammed by the autoloader. Once loaded, the gun may return to its previous position. Although it is possible for the autoloader to complete the loading cycle in as little as eight seconds, the carousel only turns in one direction: therefore, it can take as many as fifteen seconds if the next round is in a bad position, which will require the carousel to traverse through its entire magazine before finding the requested round.<br /> <br /> While the autoloader is held to be reliable, if the tank's hydraulic power to it is lost or disabled, the autoloader can be cranked by hand to supply power. However, this is a very slow process, and the gunner cannot access the rounds and feed them by hand: manual loading may be done by the tank commander turning a pair of cranks to force the machinery to move. This process takes 22 turns, minus the commander's Stamina dots.<br /> <br /> Although legends persist of the T-72's autoloader &quot;chopping off the gunner's arm and stuffing it into the breech&quot;, this is a myth. The T-72's autoloader consists of a cassette and rammer rather than a &quot;mechanical arm&quot; as these legends sometimes suggest. While the moving machinery is unprotected and presents numerous dangerous pinch points which can <i>definitely</i> remove a limb if one were to be inserted into the machinery, this is typical for <i>all</i> tanks, rather than being exclusive to Soviet ones. To actually load a crewmember's arm into the breech would require the gunner or commander to reach over over the autoloader, into the path of the dangerous machinery <i>before</i> pushing the button. Nevertheless, the interior of the T-72 is extremely cramped and uncomfortable, which contributes to crew fatigue.<br /> <br /> The gunner has access to an 1A40 gunsight complex and fire control system and TPNZ-49 night sight, which automatically measures the lead angle of a target, and the commander has a K10T collimator sight which includes a roof-mounted infrared sight. A searchlight is mounted on the front of the turret, on the right side of the gun (from the commander's perspective) which can double as both an infrared search light and a visible light search light through the use of a filter.<br /> <br /> The hull of the T-72 is built from rolled homogenous steel armour and made by welding, and the distinctive dome-shaped turret is produced by casting. On the T-72A, there is a combination of fiberglass or silica plates set behind the front glacis and near the front of the turret, intended to act as an early composite armour.<br /> <br /> In the west, the T-72A has the reporting name of &quot;Dolly Parton.&quot;<br /> <br /> The T-72 would compete with the T-64 and the later model T-80 as the main battle tank of the Soviet Union, and later with the newer T-90s. Despite the huge variety of tanks that the Soviet Union and its successor states, namely Russia procured, the T-72 ended up being being produced well into the late 1990's, and modernized versions, the T-72B3 obr.2016 started being delivered to the Russian Ground Forces in 2017. According to Jane's Information Group in 2019, the T-72 is likely to remain in service indefinitely; even wealthier countries that use the T-72 or any of its variants are likely to keep them in service for generations to come.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Variants</u></b><br /> <br /> <b>T-72B</b><br /> <br /> The T-72B or &quot;Super Dolly Parton&quot; is an improved modification of the T-72 which entered service in 1988 after being introduced in 1985. The main difference between the T-72B and T-72A is the addition of Kontakt-1 explosive reactive armour bricks attached to every surface possible over the tank. The T-72B gains ERA armour that provides an additional 400mmRHAe protection against HEAT, HESH, and HE shells, for an additional +61 of armour, with the usual rules of ERA applying. However, the Kontakt-1 explosive reactive armour bricks are useless against kinetic shells: this durability is reduced to zero versus kinetic shells. Replace the engine with V-84M V-12, generating 840 horsepower, and increase the curb weight to 44,500kg.<br /> <br /> Unlike its predecessor, the T-72B has the equipment necessary to fire guided missiles from its main gun.<br /> <br /> <b>T-72B3 obr.2016</b><br /> <br /> Following a prolonged period of financial problems that reached a boiling point at the end of the 1990's, the Russian Ministry of Defence could no longer afford to purchase T-90s, which resulted in the Ministry of Defence opting to instead upgrade some of its reserve T-72BM tanks that had been mothballed since the dissolution of the USSR. The upgrade started in 2011, and by 2016, the first units were issued to the Russian Ground Forces. Since then, the T-72B3 obr.2016 has become the most numerous and upgraded T-72 version in the Russian Ground Forces.<br /> <br /> The T-72B3, and its successor, the T-72B3 obr.2016 removes the search light and the Kontakt-1 explosive reactive armour bricks, and replaces them with the Kontakt-5 &quot;wedges&quot; around the circumference of the turret which provides an additional +61 ERA Durability to the turret front, sides, the hull sides and front glacis, applying equally to explosive rounds, and half to kinetic rounds, with all the usual limitations of single-use ERA bricks. The searchlight was deemed unnecessary and eliminated to make room for the Kontakt-5 modules.<br /> <br /> Replace the gun with the 2A46-5, allowing the T-72B3 to fire the same ammunition as the T-90, mirroring its Gun Stability Rating to allow it to fire on the move. The T-72B3 obr.2016 is outfitted with the 3EC13-1 fire extinguishing system and has the V-92SF diesel engine, which increases output 1,130hp (850kW). Increase curb weight to 46,000kg.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Special Rules</u></b><br /> <br /> <b>Poor Reversing:</b> Many Soviet and Russian tanks do not have more than one reverse gear, and have a very slow reversing speed. Tanks with this rule are limited to 1/10th of their overall speed when driving in reverse.<br /> <br /> <b>Autoloader:</b> The T-72 relies on the use of an electrically driven autoloader for its main weapon. This move eliminates the loader's station. Rounds are stored centrally underneath the turret basket, in the hull of the vehicle, where the machine selects them, elevates them up into the gun breech, then positions and loads them. The autoloader extracts the stub from the gun breech and tosses it out of a small hatch in the back of the turret so the process can be restarted. Although both Eastern and Western philosophy disagrees on the use of autoloaders, the use of tank autoloaders automates the process of reloading the main gun and saves considerable amounts of space compared to a human loader.<br /> <br /> It takes 3 turns for an autoloader to finish its cycle. If initiative for the autoloader must be determined, assume that the autoloader functions at the height of the initiative order. Once a round has been loaded by the autoloader, the machine cannot take it back out and stow the round, it must be either fired or ejected.<br /> <br /> Both components of the autoloader must be in functioning order for it to work. Should either the autoloader components in the turret or hull be reduced to 0 structure, the autoloader is destroyed and the main gun cannot be used.<br /> <br /> <b>Poor Gun Depression:</b> Soviet doctrine held that tanks would be fighting in big, wide open spaces, and that tanks would be handling offensives while charging forward on the move, rather than fighting defensively while taking cover behind a hill. As a result, Soviet analysts deemed that trading a large turret which would have allowed for more gun depression for a smaller turret which offered a lower profile for less target presence and less weight would be a net gain.<br /> <br /> This has the effect of making the turret smaller and more compact, which was advantageous during the Cold War, prior to the days of computer-assisted aiming. However, the lower turret meant that the large and bulky gun breech would have no space to travel opposite the barrel. Tanks with this rule have an extremely limited arc that they may lower their gun barrel through. This seriously hinders the tank's ability to fight from a defensive position or in defilade.<br /> <br /> Vehicles with this rule cannot fight effectively from a hull-down position. When going hull-down in any position that was not purposely set up for the tank such as a concrete barrier sized appropriately to the height of the tank's turret ring, the tank's main weapon suffers a -5 penalty to hit. This stacks with any other modifiers and may exceed the -5 maximum.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Modifications</u></b><br /> <br /> <b>Improvised Roof Armour:</b> During the 2022 Invasion of Ukraine, some Russian tanks were seen with what appeared to be slat armour bolted onto the top of the tank. Many of them appeared to have been improvised and made from structural steel or corrugated metal or even sections of chain link fence. These were nicknamed kozyrek ot solntsa or \u201csun visors\u201d, but detractors on the internet mocked them as \u201ccope cages\u201d following some reports of the improvised armour being ineffective at increasing protection of the tank\u2019s roof versus HEAT rounds and Javelin antitank guided missiles.<br /> <br /> Mechanics: A centimetre or roughly a half inch of structural steel armour spaced approximately a metre from the tank, if done correctly and under ideal circumstances, has the potential to add up to Durability 4 (Spaced) over the tank\u2019s roof. The GM secretly rolls the maker\u2019s Intelligence + Crafts skill, which suffers a -3 penalty if the designer does not have any specialty in modern armour design, antitank weaponry, or similar. Successes, to a maximum of 4 decide how much armour with the Spaced modifier is gained. On a failure, no benefit is gained but the drawbacks still apply. The amendment for non-ballistic grade steel is factored into these numbers. Penalties apply for inadequate materials. The use of these improvised armour cages preclude the use of the remote weapon stations, and limit any weapons mounted on the commander\u2019s hatch to being able to fire only directly forward and at ground level. Lastly, they increase the amount of time taken to enter or exit the vehicle by an extra turn for any of the turret crew.<br /> </div> </div> </div>Information, fluff and special rules for the 2A46 family of 125mm Smoothbore Guns is included in the PDF. This ammunition selection applies to the T-72A, which lacked the capability to fire guided missiles from its gun. It's been posted here for a preview, as well as the 3OF26 HE round.<br /> <br /> The 3VBM9 APFSDS-T will do 32 AP30 damage on an average roll of 1d10. This is by design: it simply means that given our baseline given by the armour chart in the section &quot;Vehicle Armour&quot;, around half of the time, the sabot will penetrate a solid wall of high strength, hardened steel approximately 170mm or just shy of 7&quot; thick, inflicting 8 damage dice in spalling when it does on anyone within a radius of 3m, 4 damage dice on anyone within an arc of 6m, and 2 damage dice for anyone within 12m. If you play without the rule for enhanced lateral effects, it will on average inflict an average of 3 Lethal Damage on everyone three metres on the other side of penetration when it does. If you play with the rule for enhanced lateral effects on a tank sabot (immediately count half the spall dice as successes, add inferno rule for affected blast area) it will do an average of 5 Lethal Damage, 3 Lethal Damage, and 1 Lethal Damage respectively in the same arcs as well as cause the Inferno tilt, which can then set fire to and cause the fuel and ammunition stored in the target vehicle struck by the sabot to explode. Spalling Damage is limited by the Durability of the object struck: firing the 3VBM9 APFSDS round at the windshield of a humble passenger car, which will likely have nothing more than a thin sheet of tempered glass protecting the passengers rated Durability 1, will simply result in the round passing through the car by going in one side and out the other without any lateral effects, provided the penetrator doesn't actually hit any of the passengers on the way through.<br /> <br /> 32 AP30 damage may sound like an insane amount considering the most powerful weapon in Chronicles of Darkness Core Rulebook is the Chainsaw at 5L 9-again AP0, but bear in mind, we are talking about a <i>tank gun</i> from the Cold War: a .44 Magnum, which is suggested as a 2-Damage &quot;Heavy Revolver&quot; [Chronicles of Darkness, Core Rulebook, P.268] firing a 240-grain jacketed hard cast lead bullet at close to 1,300 feet per second won't even put one dent in a 10mm thick AR-500 hardened steel plate (&quot;Abrasion Resistant&quot; steel, commonly used to line the inside of dump trucks or earth movers) at half that distance. Modern tank guns must sometimes penetrate combinations of high-strength hardened steel plates, ceramic, voids, and ultra-hard materials like tungsten or depleted uranium that work in concert with each other to add up to the equivalence of a <i>metre</i> of hardened high-strength steel plate, from two kilometres away! Everything is relative.<br /> <br /> I understand that an APFSDS round that hits tank armour will typically break itself and the armour plates they hit into at least a bucket-sized amount of buckshot-sized pellets going at multiple kilometres per second, each of which will be glowing hot from the friction of entry. However, I deliberately chose to make spalling a tiny bit weaker than it would have normally been suggested to to allow for crews to have some odds of surviving a direct penetrating hit to their tank grievously wounded but alive. This was one of the rare times I decided to allow gameplay and conservation of drama to supercede a gameplay choice, but it was deliberate on my part.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <br /> <b><u>2A46-1 125mm L/48 Smoothbore Gun (1976)</u></b><br /> <br /> <b>Damage:</b> 1d10+26 AP* (dependent on shell choice)<br /> <b>Calibre:</b> 125\u00d7383mm (Two part)<br /> <b>Range:</b> PB/1250/2500/5000<br /> <b>Accuracy:</b> 5/7/10/15<br /> <b>TDD:</b> 1/1/1/2<br /> <b>Reload:</b> 3 Turns (Autoloader)<br /> <b>Crew:</b> 1<br /> <br /> <b>3BK12M HEAT-FS: High Explosive Anti-Tank, Fin-Stabilised</b><br /> <b>Damage:</b> -7 +AP46<br /> <b>Force:</b> 6+6d AP4 (not cumulative with Damage)<br /> <b>Blast:</b> 15/30/60<br /> <b>DDO:</b> 0/0/0/0<br /> <br /> <b>3OF26 HE: High Explosive</b><br /> <b>Force:</b> 6+6d AP4<br /> <b>Blast:</b> 20/40/80<br /> <b>DDO:</b> 0/0/0/0<br /> <br /> <b>3VBM7 APFSDS-T: Armour-Piercing Fin-Stabilised Discarding Sabot, Tracer</b><br /> The 3VBM7 changed the performance of earlier high-strength maraging steel penetrators slightly by adding a longer and slightly heavier projectile to improve performance, and was issued in 1972.<br /> <b>Damage:</b> +AP23<br /> <b>DDO:</b> 0/0/-2/-4<br /> <br /> <b>3VBM9 APFSDS-T: Armour-Piercing Fin-Stabilised Discarding Sabot, Tracer</b><br /> In 1976, the penetrator of the 3VBM7 APFSDS-T was completely redesigned, with a steel-encased tungsten carbide penetrator and redesigned geometry. Entered service in 1976.<br /> <b>Damage:</b> +AP30<br /> <b>DDO:</b> 0/0/-2/-4<br /> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My Project to convert Chronicles of Darkness to a data-driven <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd\" target=\"_blank\">Combined Arms Simulation: Gods of Iron</a><br /> My personal website, projects and games: <a href=\"https://puckerfactorgames.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://puckerfactorgames.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","name":"Jester-PFG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27707_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-17T18:33:16-08:00","id":1499997,"title":"","content":"You're very detail-oriented and technical.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"740"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-18T00:25:17-08:00","id":1500016,"title":"","content":"Thanks, I hope that this might be useful someday.<br /> <br /> I'm going to shift gears and work on a different area besides tanks for next update. I work on this as time permits, but I should have more content once things settle down in the new year.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My Project to convert Chronicles of Darkness to a data-driven <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd\" target=\"_blank\">Combined Arms Simulation: Gods of Iron</a><br /> My personal website, projects and games: <a href=\"https://puckerfactorgames.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://puckerfactorgames.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","name":"Jester-PFG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27707_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-01-29T12:14:42-08:00","id":1501632,"title":"","content":"Hope everybody had a great holiday!<br /> <br /> As of this update, in Chapter 5.5: Light Machine Guns, I've added mechanics for <b>The Beaten Zone</b>, as well as searching and traversing fire, which is applicable for use with machine guns with <i>traverse and elevation</i> mechanisms, as well as mechanics for <i>grazing fire</i>. This adds an extra dimenison for machine guns in game which very few games capture. For more details, I've included a link to a Marine Corps Tactical Publication on machine guns and machine gunnery which should help to explain what The Beaten Zone, Searching and Traversing Fire, and Grazing Fire is in the footnotes of this post [1].<br /> <br /> I owe a deep debt of gratitude to one of my subject-matter experts for explaining to me that machine guns have considerably greater tactical applications than may initially meet the eye, and machine gunnery is to itself a considerable science, more akin to artillery. These rules are at this moment untested, so I'll be playtesting them later and making edits and adjustments as necessary.<br /> <br /> Chapter 7.1.3 as of the time of writing now features a small chapter on strategic-level warfare. I got the idea after exploring <i>ICBM</i> by Matrix Games[2], and decided I could use that in my table, since my table has routinely discussed the implications of a nuclear weapon in use. I developed a number of (extremely simplified) procedures for simulating the process of nuclear warfare at an RPG table and the &quot;fog of war&quot; inherent to the situation, which involve writing your plans onto notes which get placed in the center of the table, and then the notes being read off once everyone's committed. This is intended to capture the game theory involved with a first strike and launch-on-warning capabilities, as well as the value of survivable second-strike capabilities.<br /> <br /> Currently, I only have mechanics for the Trident II and the Minuteman III in service with the United States' Navy and Air Force respectively, but I may add the RS-24 Yars, which is intended to replace the RT-2PMP Topol-M, as well as the R-36 ICBM, and R-56 Bulava and R-29 Vyosota at a later date. In the future I intend to add the Titan and the LGM-118A Peacekeeper, for the &quot;what if&quot; crowd. However, I'm planning to spin off all of the air and naval warfare stuff into a separate book, since any RPG supplement longer than 200 pages starts to get a little bit cumbersome and I haven't even gotten onto IFVs at this stage. I've included some rudimentary figures for some other nuclear weapons in the USN's and USAF's stockpile, which I've obtained from Stardestroyer.net's nuclear weapons effects calculator[4]. Currently, I haven't included any ballistic missile defence systems (such as the Aegis Ballistic Missile Defence System) into the book, but I could at a later date. I'm somewhat hesitant to go that route because actual data on ballistic missile defence systems is somewhat scarce and I would have to do a lot of guessing to get them represented.<br /> <br /> As always, any comments, questions and feedback that could help me produce a better supplement are welcomed.<br /> <br /> <br /> [1] <i>Marine Corps Tactical Publication (MCTP) 3-01C, Machine Guns and Machine Gun Gunnery.</i> - 1-12 (Page 22 of the PDF at this time) - <a href=\"https://www.marines.mil/News/Publications/MCPEL/Electronic-Library-Display/Article/900523/mctp-3-01c/\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.marines.mil/News/Publica...23/mctp-3-01c/</a><br /> <br /> [2] <i>&quot;ICBM&quot;</i> - Matrix Games: <a href=\"https://www.matrixgames.com/game/icbm\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.matrixgames.com/game/icbm</a><br /> <br /> [3] <i>NUKEMAP by Alex Wellerstein</i> - <a href=\"https://nuclearsecrecy.com/nukemap/\" target=\"_blank\">https://nuclearsecrecy.com/nukemap/</a><br /> <br /> [4] <i>Star Wars vs Star Trek: Nuclear Weapon Effects Calcuator</i> by Michael Wong - <a href=\"http://www.stardestroyer.net/Empire/Science/Nuke.html\u200b\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.stardestroyer.net/Empire/Science/Nuke.html\u200b</a>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My Project to convert Chronicles of Darkness to a data-driven <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd\" target=\"_blank\">Combined Arms Simulation: Gods of Iron</a><br /> My personal website, projects and games: <a href=\"https://puckerfactorgames.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://puckerfactorgames.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","name":"Jester-PFG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27707_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-03-03T18:42:38-08:00","id":1504309,"title":"","content":"Have you ever seen the movie, <i>White Tiger</i>? Inspirational.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"740"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-03-17T20:41:58-07:00","id":1505631,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Iguazu</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd?p=1504309#post1504309\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Have you ever seen the movie, <i>White Tiger</i>? Inspirational.</div> </div> </div> </div>Absolutely! I've actually listed it on (at the time of posting) Page 14 of the PDF, under &quot;Inspirational Sources.&quot; It's not only got tank action, but it also aligns with how I would want to portray the supernatural alongside tanks and war when GM'ing for Chronicles of Darkness. The way in particular that it portrayed Naydenov in particular was huge for me and I think that's what I would want to aim for in terms of feel and aesthetic to the campaign.<br /> <br /> <i>White Tiger</i> was actually one of the sources of inspiration for <i>Gods of Iron</i> and one of the reasons I decided to include some Second World War era stuff. If you're curious, I have the Tiger Ausf B. Konigstiger (the &quot;King Tiger&quot; or Tiger II, or rather, the Tiger I's eventual successor) in <i>Gods of Iron</i>. I don't have the T-34-85 or the Tiger I yet, but I could add those later. At the time of posting, the King Tiger is on Page 203 of Gods of Iron, under &quot;Second World War Heavy Tanks&quot;; I included it and the Sherman because at the time I had been doing some reading on The Battle of the Bulge.<br /> <br /> Things have been a bit slow because I haven't had as much free time as I had before, but I've recently been considering moving the sections on stuff for naval and air operations into their own books, which I want to call <i>Seas of Steel</i> and <i>Angels and Aces</i> respectively and focus on those at a later point in time, since I feel like I should try to avoid splitting my focus and the book's already more than 200 pages long.\u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My Project to convert Chronicles of Darkness to a data-driven <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd\" target=\"_blank\">Combined Arms Simulation: Gods of Iron</a><br /> My personal website, projects and games: <a href=\"https://puckerfactorgames.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://puckerfactorgames.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","name":"Jester-PFG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27707_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-22T15:35:24-07:00","id":1509321,"title":"","content":"I know it's been a while since I updated this, but this weekend I was able to scrape together a small update. I promised combined arms warfare, so I'm working on delivering some of that rather than just straight-up tanks, so we can have some more diverse options in gameplay.<br /> <br /> I just updated the document today and added the M3 Bradley.<br /> <br /> I've added a section on IFVs, and I decided to add the M3 Bradley and all of its weapons. Unlike the other additions that I used here, the numbers for the M242 Bushmaster and the BGM-71E should be more or less good, rather than placeholder values.<br /> <br /> Even if combined arms warfare isn't in your Chronicles' focus, I think that more player groups and GMs will find more use for IFVs rather than tanks, so I decided to focus on that and add this. I now have stats for the M3A4 Bradley (which can be worked backwards to get stats for the older M2 Bradley) represented in Chronicles of Darkness.<br /> <br /> Hope you like it, I've posted the excerpt below.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b><u>M3A4 Bradley</u></b><br /> <b>Curb Weight:</b> 27,600 kg<br /> <b>Powerplant:</b> Cummins VTA-903T Turbodiesel (675 bhp, 500kW)<br /> <b>Maximum Speed:</b> 65km/h (54 metres per turn)<br /> <b>Acceleration:</b> 6 metres per turn, per turn<br /> <b>Handling:</b> -2<br /> <b>Terrain Rating:</b> 5 (max)/ 6 (half)/ 8 (quarter)<br /> <b>Tactical Diameter:</b> Neutral, 0+(speed+1)/2<br /> <b>TTS:</b> 1/1<br /> <b>Gun Stabilisation:</b> -10<br /> <b>Crew:</b> 3: Commander, Driver, Gunner + 2 Passengers<br /> <b>Fuel Capacity:</b> 660L<br /> <b>Range:</b> 500km (est) on road<br /> <b>Armament:</b> 1\u00d7 25mm M242 Chain Gun (900 rounds), 2\u00d7 BGM-71E TOW-2 anti-tank missile tubes (12 rounds total), 1\u00d7 7.62\u00d751mm M240C Machine Gun (2,200 rounds)<br /> <br /> The M2 Bradley Infantry Fighting Vehicle entered service in December 1981 as a replacement for the US Army's M113 Track which was starting to overstay its welcome along with the M60 Patton. Designed without weapons and with a much older powerplant and transmission, the M113 Track could keep pace with the M60 Patton it was designed to accompany into battle, but not with the newer M1 Abrams MBT that was replacing the Army's faithful go-to. After a long and slightly troubled development history, the Army selected a design tabled by United Defense and named it after General Omar Bradley.<br /> <br /> United Defense took a new approach to designing the M2 Bradley, and built it resembling a small tank of sorts, with a 25mm chain gun as its main weapon, with hardpoints to accomodate other weapons, including TOW missiles, and rounding out the main gun with an M240G mounted machine gun. Additional ports for firing port weapons allowed the passengers to add to the Bradley's arsenal, although these were found to paradoxically \\textit{decrease} survivability and were eliminated on later versions.<br /> <br /> The M2 Bradley was one of the first vehicles to use aluminium in its armour. This was a move that came with considerable controversy; although the armour was capable of standing up to the 14.5mm armour piercing rounds that the Soviet BMP fired, the armour was also liable to burn at high temperatures, which was blamed on some of the casualties that the Bradley sustained. Nevertheless, the Bradley proved to be considerably more survivable than wheeled LAV (Light Armoured Vehicle).<br /> <br /> In response to complaints about insufficient survivability, subsequent versions of the M2 Bradley did come with hardened steel side skirts and armour which increased the weight of the platform, and required the engine to be upgraded to produce 600 horsepower to maintain the Bradley's desired speed and performance. This became the M3 Bradley, which is depicted here.<br /> <br /> The M2 Bradley would later gain fame in the Gulf War, especially during the Battle of 73 Easting, the largest tank battle since the Second World War, in the coalition response to the Iraqi invasion of Kuwait. Bradleys used their TOW missiles to engage the Iraqi Republican Guard T-72s, and scored more kills on Iraqi T-72s than the M1A2 Abrams did.<br /> <br /> Although the Bradley has been arguably a successful design, it has not been without considerable amounts of controversy; the cramped interior has been cited in reducing the effectiveness of the crew and the passengers, and some have called the M2 Bradley &quot;a jack of all trades but a master of none&quot; citing the limited number of TOW missiles it can carry, as well as the IFV's great weight and relative lack of survivability. Efforts have been underway to replace the M2 Bradley since the late 1980's, although so far only in the late 2010s were serious efforts finally made to replace it with the Next Generation Combat Vehicle. The M3 Bradley was meant to address a number of these shortcomings, although it did so by giving up room for passengers as well as its ability to mount a floatation curtain for deep fording.<br /> <br /> The M2A3 replaced some of the Bradley's analogue optics and analogue fire control system with an all-digital fire control and weapons system, which continues to see service on the newer M3 Bradleys. It's very likely that the Bradley, while its replacement has been talked about for over forty years, will continue to serve in the US Armed Forces (as well as a number of other militaries that the M2 Bradley has been exported to) for the foreseeable future. The Bradley will likely be exported to Ukraine as part of foreign aid to assist Ukraine in the Russo-Ukrainian war.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Armour and Systems</u></b><br /> <br /> Size / Durability / Structure<br /> <br /> <b>Turret</b><br /> Front (Aluminium, 50mm RHAe) (7 / 13 Cp / 20)<br /> Sides (Aluminium, 50mm RHAe) (- / 13 Cp / -)<br /> Rear (Aluminium, 50mm RHAe) (- / 13 Cp / -)<br /> Roof (Aluminium, 30mm RHAe) (- / 10 / -)<br /> <br /> <b>Hull</b><br /> Glacis (Aluminium, 50mm RHAe) (16 / 13 Cp / 29)<br /> Sides (Aluminium, 50mm RHAe) (- / 13 Cp / -)<br /> Rear (Aluminium, 50mm RHAe) (- / 13 Cp / -)<br /> Belly (Aluminium, 50mm, RHAe) (- / 13 Cp / -)<br /> Roof over engine deck (30mm RHAe) (- / 13 Cp / -)<br /> Tracks (7 / 10 / 17)<br /> <br /> <b>Systems</b><br /> Engine (in hull) (7 / 3 / 10)<br /> Ammunition Hold (all rounds in hull) (5 / 1 / 6)<br /> Fuel Tanks (in hull) (5 / 1 / 6)<br /> Gun Breech (in turret) (5 / 3 / 8)<br /> <br /> <br /> <b><u>Variants</u></b><br /> <br /> <b>M2 Bradley</b><br /> <br /> The original M2 Bradley carried more room for passengers (although by no stretch of the imagination in comfort) as well as carried room for firing ports (see below).<br /> <br /> <i>Mechanics:</i> Eliminate all but 2 of the TOW missiles, and increase passenger space to accomodate up to 6 passengers. Reduce armour to 40mm RHAe, or Durability 12.<br /> <br /> <b>M6 Linebacker</b><br /> <br /> In the late 2010's, studies were done to see if the Bradley chassis could be converted to a short-range air defence platform citing the emergence of drones and smaller airborne threats.<br /> <br /> [b]Mechanics:} Replace the 25mm Autocannon and the M240C machine gun with an XM914 30mm Autocannon firing self-destroying rounds. Remove the TOW missiles and replace them with either the FIM-92 Stinger or the AIM-9X Sidewinder missile. The vehicle gains the &quot;Anti-Air&quot; rule for these weapons but the FIM-92 and the AIM-9X sidewinder cannot be used to target ground targets.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Special Rules</u></b><br /> <br /> <b>Firing Ports</b><br /> <br /> Only available on the M2 Bradley<br /> <br /> The M2 Bradley originally was designed with a number of ball mounted ports and vision blocks from which the crew could use to fire from the sides and rear door of the vehicle with specially equipped carbines cut down to work as firing port weapons, following some experimental tests and data gathered from experiences in the Vietnam War.<br /> <br /> The M2 Bradley's Firing Port weapons may be fired to the side facings or out the rear door only and must be operated by the passengers. A -1 penalty is observed when using them and a minimum range of 3m applies, below which is an automatic chance roll. The M231 Bradley Firing Port Weapon resembles a small, open-bolt M4 Carbine with a mounting interface in place of the handguards. Due to the lack of sights, the operating procedure for the FPW was to point the gun in the direction of the target and walk a spray of bullets onto it while viewing the spray through a cramped periscope on the roof, and the gun's painfully high rate of fire thanks to a series of modifications which included replacement of the fire control group with an open-bolt fire control group and small magazine size made this difficult. The FPW can only fire medium and long bursts and always counts as firing from the hip; the ball turret precludes the use of sights.<br /> <br /> Although they looked good &quot;on paper&quot; experiences in combat quickly showed that these firing port weapons proved to be less than ideal on a two-way range when missiles and rockets entered the fray. Troops often paradoxically survived for longer (allowing them to remain effective in battle for longer) if they could get \\textit{out} of their vehicle as quickly as they could and engage enemy forces on foot, rather than try to return fire from the confines of an extremely cooped up and cramped vehicle that carried an enormous target presence for incoming rockets that its armour could not defeat. As a result, most of the M2 Bradleys at the present day have had their firing ports permanently removed and blanked off with metal plates welded overtop to eliminate the weak points in the armour left by the ball turrets. The M3 Bradleys lack firing ports entirely.<br /> <br /> The Bradley's firing ports are considered a size 1 weak spot. However, they are only Durability 4, Structure 5, and they can be shot out if directly targeted.<br /> <br /> <b>V-Belly</b><br /> In response to casualty rates resulting from bombs and landmines in Vietnam, the M2 Bradley was outfitted a V-Shaped hull that would try to deflect the blast of an explosive away from the vulnerable belly of the vehicle and out its sides. This increased the chances that the vehicle or the crew would survive if it rolled over a bomb.<br /> <br /> Count the vehicle as being 25\\% smaller than it normally is (round in favour of the vehicle) when calculating the damage it takes from an explosion from a landmine or an IED.<br /> <br /> <b>Smoke Launchers</b><br /> Each Bradley carries several four-barrelled smoke launchers on the perimeter of the turret which can be fired from the commander's station to lay down cover of smoke to obscure the vehicle on the battlefield.<br /> <br /> At any point as a Reflexive Action, the commander may choose to cover the area surrounding the vehicle by deploying the smoke launchers. The area around the vehicle, within a 20m radius counts as being in darkness, even to infrared vision, as the smoke generated by white phosphorous smoke generators is opaque even to infrared light.<br /> <br /> <b><u>US M242 Bushmaster</u></b><br /> <b>Damage:</b> 7+3d AP*<br /> <b>Calibre:</b> 25\u00d7137mm<br /> <b>Range:</b> PB/1500/3000/6000<br /> <b>Accuracy:</b> PB/5/7/10<br /> <b>TDD:</b> PB/1/1/1<br /> <b>CEP:</b> T<br /> <b>Magazine:</b> Varies<br /> <b>Rate of Fire:</b> 500 rounds/min<br /> <b>Weight:</b> 260lbs.<br /> <b>Special Rules:</b> Autofire<br /> <br /> The M242 Bushmaster Chain Cannon was developed by Hughest Helicopters in the 1980's. Development started when it was clear that the old M113 &quot;Track&quot; Armoured Personnel Carriers, while they were good at taxiing soldiers to the battlefield and protecting them from fragments and small arms fire en route to it, could contribute little to the battle beyond having a machine gun or two under some circumstances.<br /> <br /> The US Army tabled specifications for a vehicle that would function as the M113 did as an armoured troop carrier that could not only carry troops to the battlefield, but also support them once they got there with a weapon similar to the older 20mm aircraft cannons that saw use during the Second World War. The older M139 Autocannon that had served in this role was unsatisfactory and the Army put out a contract to build a new one.<br /> <br /> Hughes Helicopters developed the M242 Bushmaster. Unlike the older M139 Autocannon, the Bushmaster used a powerful electric motor to drive the cannon's mechanism, which consists of a large, sprocketed chain that carries the individual rounds to the breech of the gun and actuates it, and even allow for rounds to be switched between different types on the fly. Hughes designed it to be reliable, simple, and easy to maintain, with the assembly coming apart into separate modular sub-assemblies that can be dismantled and reassembled quickly. In an emergency, the weapon can be hand-cranked to fire, although at a much slower rate; the weapon may only fire short bursts when hand cranked.<br /> <br /> The M242 Bushmaster sees use across all branches of the US Armed Forces including the Navy and the Coast Guard as the Mark 38 Autocannon, as well as in the armed forces of Australia, Canadian, and New Zealand, and many other militaries.<br /> <br /> <b>M793 Target Practice, Tracer</b><br /> The M793 Target Practice round contains no explosive filler.<br /> <b>DDO:</b> 0/-1/-2/-4<br /> <b>M791 Armour-Piercing, Tracer</b><br /> The M791 Armour-Piercing round contains an armour piercing sabot, and is<br /> intended to penetrate light to moderate vehicle armour.<br /> <b>Damage:</b> -2<br /> <b>AP:</b> +12<br /> <b>DDO:</b> 0/-3/-5/-7<br /> <b>M792 High Explosive Incendiary, Tracer</b><br /> The M792 HEI round carries an explosive charge to increase its damage<br /> potential against lightly armoured targets and enemy infantry.<br /> <b>Damage:</b> +LV<br /> <b>Force:</b> 4+4d AP4<br /> <b>Blast:</b> 2/4/8<br /> <b>DDO:</b> 0/-1/-2/-4<br /> Incendiary. Should the M792 High Explosive Incendiary round deal any damage to flammable materials, accelerants, fuel or ammunition via its blast force, roll a number of dice equal to the damage inflicted. On a success, it ignites.<br /> <b>M919 Armour-Piercing Fin Stabilised Discarding Sabot</b><br /> The M919 APFSDS lacks a tracer, and instead uses a penetrator made from depleted uranium.<br /> <b>Damage:</b> -2<br /> <b>AP:</b> +15<br /> <b>DDO:</b> 0/-3/-5/-7<br /> As is the case with depleted uranium rounds, the rounds will explode into flames should they strike hard armour plates, and thus carry the incendiary rule.<br /> <br /> <b>BGM-71E TOW2</b><br /> <b>Damage:</b> 1d10+10 AP80<br /> <b>Force:</b> 6+6d<br /> <b>Blast:</b> 5/10/20<br /> <b>Speed:</b> 720km/h (600m per turn)<br /> <b>Range:</b> 100/MR/4000<br /> <b>Accuracy:</b> 15<br /> <b>TDD:</b> 1<br /> <b>CEP:</b> T<br /> <b>Flight Height:</b> Direct-Fired<br /> <b>Weight:</b> 50lbs.<br /> <b>Size:</b> 5<br /> <br /> The BGM-71 Tube-launched, Optically-tracked, Wire-guided or &quot;TOW&quot; missile is an anti-tank guided missile that first saw service in the 1970's with the United States Army in the Vietnam War and traces its heritage back to some experimental weapons developed by Nazi Germany which placed wire guidance onto rockets to turn them into tethered missiles, intending them to be fired from bombers.<br /> <br /> By the end of the 1950's, the novel concept of using a spool of ultrafine wire to transmit missile guidance commands to a missile had caught on. The MGM-51 Shillelagh Missile, which despite being technically advanced and highly ambitious, had several problems with expense, reliability, and subject to issues with IR interference from flares and countermeasures. The newer weapon was intended to be less expensive and more reliable.<br /> <br /> The Ballistics Research Laboratory created the acronym &quot;TOW&quot; and development progressed over the 1960's, and Hughes Helicopters won the contract to produce what became the BGM-71 TOW; this missile would weigh about fifty pounds and to facilitate launch from both helicopters and land based platforms including on vehicles and on tripods, the missile would have small, spring-loaded fins that would extend when it launched. The first missiles carried a 4-kilogram high explosive charge; this was later replaced by a 6-kilogram high explosive antitank warhead which was billed as being capable of punching through 900mm of rolled homogeneous steel armour plate, but subsequent evaluations have placed this to be significantly lower in the realm of 600mm RHAe.<br /> <br /> The TOW missile is guided to its target via optics, and the gunner must keep the sights on the target in order to steer the missile toward it until it hits, which means that the missile is not fire-and-forget. For this reason, the dice are rolled only at the moment of impact, and should the gunner move or apply defence while the missile is in flight, roll a chance die; only on a success can the missile hit or be continued to be aimed on a subsequent turn.<br /> <br /> In real life, there is a small delay between when the launch command is sent to the missile, and the missile actually launches; the missile must activate its onboard internal battery and guidance system. For this reason, TOW missiles fired at ranges within 600m are resolved last in the turn, after any other weapons and artillery systems have been resolved. </div> </div> </div>\u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My Project to convert Chronicles of Darkness to a data-driven <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd\" target=\"_blank\">Combined Arms Simulation: Gods of Iron</a><br /> My personal website, projects and games: <a href=\"https://puckerfactorgames.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://puckerfactorgames.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","name":"Jester-PFG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27707_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-17T23:10:44-07:00","id":1518215,"title":"","content":"Hello again,<br /> <br /> Just a heads up that I haven't abandoned this endeavour, I've just been very busy lately. A lot has happened since the last update, I've restructured the entire document, which included spinning off several of the entries and rules into their own LaTeX files for numerous entries (I <i>never</i> want to do a 22-Branch Octopus Merge <i>ever</i> again as long as I live, for as long as I have to) as well as reorganizing the way the document is organized to make it easier to read. I've moved most of the sections on vehicles into their own part, to make things read more coherently.<br /> <br /> Someone in another thread had suggested that they were interested in doing siege engines for <i>Chronicles of Darkness</i> and in particular doing Mediaeval siege engines for <i>Genius: The Transgression</i>, so I decided to add some stuff for you this update, it's near the end.<br /> <br /> My group expect to start seeing situations involving fortifications, and penetration of reinforced concrete are likely to become a factor, rather than just vehicle armour, so what I've done is I've taken the opportunity to develop penetration tables specifically for the purpose of attacking structures made from reinforced concrete, which can also be used as a guide for brick, masonry, and even granite.<br /> <br /> Once again, I used the benchmark of the .30-06 Armour Piercing (Damage 4 AP 2) to start with a base. Experimental testing done by The Ammo Channel has shown that an .30-06 M2 Armour-Piercing round [AMMOTEST: 30 Caliber M2 (30-06) Armor Piercing Penetration Tests - Military Surplus Ammo <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hzYH8ap1TDo\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hzYH8ap1TDo</a>, Jun 21, 2013, retrieved July 1st, 2023] will cleanly penetrate 4&quot; of Portland Concrete, but is stopped by 5&quot;. Due to the limitations of the Storyteller system, distance is <i>not</i> being factored into this. I then picked figures for the US Navy Mark 7 16&quot;/50-calibre Main Battery Guns firing the Mark 8 Armour Piercing Capped &quot;Superheavy&quot; shell, which at 10,000 yards, will penetrate 27.5 feet or 8.4 metres of high strength concrete [Tony DiGuilian, <i>USA 16&quot;/50 (40.6cm) Mark 7 - NavWeaps</i> <a href=\"http://www.navweaps.com/Weapons/WNUS_16-50_mk7.php\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.navweaps.com/Weapons/WNUS_16-50_mk7.php</a>, William H. Garzke, Jr. and Robert O. Dulin, Jr., <i>&quot;Battleships: United States Battleships, 1935 - 1992&quot;</i>] at an impact angle of 0 degrees.<br /> <br /> I then aligned both those figures to their Durability equivalence in Rolled Homogenous Armour under &quot;Vehicle Armour&quot;, and then created this chart via interpolation.<br /> <br /> This table isn't merged into the book yet, I have to add some other stuff but it's a preview of what's to come.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Thickness of Concrete in Feet and Metres to Durability</u></b><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_description\">Code:</div> <pre class=\"bbcode_code notranslate\" style=\"max-height:calc(30 * 1.4rem + 2 * var(--bbcode-padding) + var(--h-scrollbar-allowance));\">m (ft) Durability | m (ft) Durability | m (ft) Durability | m (ft) Durability 0.08(0.25) 4 | 3.12(10.25) 243 | 6.17(20.25) 485 | 9.22(30.25) 727 0.15(0.5) 7 | 3.2(10.5) 249 | 6.25(20.5) 491 | 9.3(30.5) 733 0.23(0.75) 13 | 3.28(10.75) 255 | 6.32(20.75) 497 | 9.37(30.75) 739 0.3(1) 19 | 3.35(11) 261 | 6.4(21) 503 | 9.45(31) 745 0.38(1.25) 25 | 3.43(11.25) 267 | 6.48(21.25) 509 | 9.53(31.25) 751 0.46(1.5) 31 | 3.51(11.5) 273 | 6.55(21.5) 515 | 9.6(31.5) 757 0.53(1.75) 37 | 3.58(11.75) 279 | 6.63(21.75) 521 | 9.68(31.75) 763 0.61(2) 43 | 3.66(12) 285 | 6.71(22) 527 | 9.75(32) 769 0.69(2.25) 49 | 3.73(12.25) 291 | 6.78(22.25) 533 | 9.83(32.25) 775 0.76(2.5) 55 | 3.81(12.5) 297 | 6.86(22.5) 539 | 9.91(32.5) 781 0.84(2.75) 61 | 3.89(12.75) 303 | 6.93(22.75) 545 | 9.98(32.75) 787 0.91(3) 67 | 3.96(13) 309 | 7.01(23) 551 | 10.06(33) 793 0.99(3.25) 74 | 4.04(13.25) 315 | 7.09(23.25) 557 | 10.13(33.25) 799 1.07(3.5) 80 | 4.11(13.5) 321 | 7.16(23.5) 563 | 10.21(33.5) 805 1.14(3.75) 86 | 4.19(13.75) 327 | 7.24(23.75) 569 | 10.29(33.75) 811 1.22(4) 92 | 4.27(14) 334 | 7.32(24) 575 | 10.36(34) 817 1.3(4.25) 98 | 4.34(14.25) 340 | 7.39(24.25) 581 | 10.44(34.25) 823 1.37(4.5) 104 | 4.42(14.5) 346 | 7.47(24.5) 587 | 10.52(34.5) 829 1.45(4.75) 110 | 4.5(14.75) 352 | 7.54(24.75) 593 | 10.59(34.75) 835 1.52(5) 116 | 4.57(15) 358 | 7.62(25) 600 | 10.67(35) 841 1.6(5.25) 122 | 4.65(15.25) 364 | 7.7(25.25) 606 | 10.74(35.25) 847 1.68(5.5) 128 | 4.72(15.5) 370 | 7.77(25.5) 612 | 10.82(35.5) 853 1.75(5.75) 134 | 4.8(15.75) 376 | 7.85(25.75) 618 | 10.9(35.75) 860 1.83(6) 140 | 4.88(16) 382 | 7.92(26) 624 | 10.97(36) 866 1.91(6.25) 146 | 4.95(16.25) 388 | 8(26.25) 630 | 11.05(36.25) 872 1.98(6.5) 152 | 5.03(16.5) 394 | 8.08(26.5) 636 | 11.13(36.5) 878 2.06(6.75) 158 | 5.11(16.75) 400 | 8.15(26.75) 642 | 11.2(36.75) 884 2.13(7) 164 | 5.18(17) 406 | 8.23(27) 648 | 11.28(37) 890 2.21(7.25) 170 | 5.26(17.25) 412 | 8.31(27.25) 654 | 11.35(37.25) 896 2.29(7.5) 176 | 5.33(17.5) 418 | 8.38(27.5) 660 | 11.43(37.5) 902 2.36(7.75) 182 | 5.41(17.75) 424 | 8.46(27.75) 666 | 11.51(37.75) 908 2.44(8) 188 | 5.49(18) 430 | 8.53(28) 672 | 11.58(38) 914 2.51(8.25) 194 | 5.56(18.25) 436 | 8.61(28.25) 678 | 11.66(38.25) 920 2.59(8.5) 200 | 5.64(18.5) 442 | 8.69(28.5) 684 | 11.73(38.5) 926 2.67(8.75) 207 | 5.72(18.75) 448 | 8.76(28.75) 690 | 11.81(38.75) 932 2.74(9) 213 | 5.79(19) 454 | 8.84(29) 696 | 11.89(39) 938 2.82(9.25) 219 | 5.87(19.25) 460 | 8.92(29.25) 702 | 11.96(39.25) 944 2.9(9.5) 225 | 5.94(19.5) 467 | 8.99(29.5) 708 | 12.04(39.5) 950 2.97(9.75) 231 | 6.02(19.75) 473 | 9.07(29.75) 714 | 12.12(39.75) 956 3.05(10) 237 | 6.1(20) 479 | 9.14(30) 720 | 12.19(40) 962</pre> </div>This chart assumes construction grade high strength Portland Concrete, which has a compressive strength of approximately 5,000 psi after curing. I understand that penetration and hardness is a huge area that multiple Ph.D level thesises could be published on, but we're doing gaming, not engineering, so I'm using it as a rough guideline to draw a few simple rules of thumbs.<br /> <br /> Different materials have different properties, and so what I've determined are the following bounds for other materials:<br /> <br /> Packed Sand - When penetrating into the earth (such as in the case of a bunker buster bomb), a quick rule of thumb is to imagine that every foot of solid reinforced concrete equals about ten feet of hard-packed earth and sand. So for instance, two feet of hard-packed sand would equal Durability 4, as opposed to two feet of reinforced concrete would equal Durability 43.<br /> <br /> Bricks - Modern construction-grade bricks typically have a compressive strength of approximately 1/5th that of high strength concrete, so we take 1/5th of the listed values if the barrier is made from bricks. For instance, a 9&quot; thick brick wall around a mansion would provide Durability 3: enough to provide some ballistic protection against an assault rifle, but a 6&quot; thick brick wall would come out to Durability 2 and could be shot through by the same assault rifle.<br /> <br /> Masonry - Stone masonry can be double the strength of bricks or greater, depending on the type and arrangement of the stones used, the fitment of the stones, and the skill of the mason. For good quality masonry, we take 2/5th of the listed value if the target is made from solid stone masonry. Note that castles were rarely solid masonry: very often, they were masonry on the outside, but were hard-packed sand on the inside (see the entry above), so this has to be taken into account when making walls of large fortifications, such as castles. Not all castles were masonry, even; some of the historic buildings and palaces in Russia are actually made of bricks and are simply covered in plaster and paint which needs to be reapplied every year.<br /> <br /> White Oak - White Oak has a compressive strength perpindicular to its grain of 1,070 psi [<a href=\"https://www.woodworkweb.com/woodwork-topics/wood/146-wood-strengths.html\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.woodworkweb.com/woodwork...strengths.html</a>, retrieved July 17th, 2023], which places it about on the order of the strength of bricks. A 2-foot solid layer of white oak armour, typical for 17th Century First Rate Line of Battle Ships (using the old British &quot;rating&quot;) system would count as Durability 8.<br /> <br /> Granite - Granite can be around three to four times the compressive strength of construction grade concrete, depending on how the concrete is reinforced and poured and the age of the concrete (it can take some time for concrete to cure to its full strength), however it's not nearly as effective at resisting penetration compared to rolled homogenous armour, mostly due to issues with it shattering when struck. Granite counts for one and a half times (x1.5) the Durability of construction grade concrete, for the equivalent thickness. So a foot of Granite would count for Durability 29, a foot of concrete would count for Durability 19, and a foot of hardened ballistic grade steel plate (300mmRHA) would count for Durability 51 [See Also: <i>Gods of Iron</i> Vehicle Armour].<br /> <br /> Round to the nearest whole number when calculating.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Composite Cover</u></b><br /> When layering armour types, take the net thickness of each individual layer, and add them together.<br /> <br /> <i>Example of Play:</i> a castle wall consisting of one foot of masonry on the outside, a foot of bricks on the inside, and four feet of sand in between would be done as the following:<br /> <br /> A foot of construction grade concrete counts for Durability 19, but the Masonry only counts as 2/5th of that, which comes to 7.6, or Durability 8.<br /> <br /> Four feet of construction grade concrete counts as Durability 92, but because it's sand, not concrete, this comes to Durability 9.<br /> <br /> Another foot of concrete would count for Durability 19, but bricks only count for 1/5th of that, which leaves us with 3.8 or Durability 4.<br /> <br /> Thus, a siege cannon would need to have a Damage output of or Damage output plus Armour Piercing quality of 21 in order to penetrate the cover. A siege cannon with Damage 10 might be able to shoot through the outer wall and penetrate the outer masonry, but the cannonballs it fires will come to a stop in the layer of packed sand inbetween the outer Masonry and the brick work.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b><u>RoadMap</u></b><br /> <br /> There are still a few major and minor items to do, so I'm setting this here to remind myself for future references and to give a bit of a &quot;next stop&quot; in the road map.<br /> <br /> - The explosives update needs to be done. I'm planning to create an explosives formula that will allow blast radii and damage/force to be calculated from explosive brisance, charge size, and the presence of fragments. I'm considering adapting one of the functions from GURPS, albeit greatly simplified.<br /> - Apply the rule of size scaling to explosive damage done, as per Armoury 1st Edition.<br /> - Mechanics for HESH rounds need to be added.<br /> - Mechanics for Degradation to armour and cover need to be added.<br /> - 30mm cannons, 25mm cannons, and 20mm cannons still have placeholder values being used in their tables. By the charts, they should have a damage base of 10, 9, and 8 respectively.<br /> - Some examples of play need to be fixed in accordance with the gameplay rules.<br /> - Virtually all the missiles need to be redone after the explosive update.<br /> - The USN Mk. 7 16&quot;/50-calibre guns are going to be redone to tune damage, as I've retrieved better tables for them. In the future for <i>Seas of Steel</i> I would like to make them take into account both deck and side armour. This will likely need a complete and total do-over.<br /> - Anti-aircraft weaponry needs to have Accuracy and TDD redone in order to prevent nonsensical situations that can arise if Acc and TDD are set to 1 and 1 respectively and common sense is not applied, such as players using an 120mm Gun M1 Superheavy Anti-Aircraft Gun to make a called shot to an enemy character's eye at a penalty of 0 at altitudes of 5,000m. (For reference, no this never happened in game, I just realized that it was possible by the &quot;letter of the rules.&quot;)<br /> <br /> <br /> <b><u>Works Cited</u></b><br /> <br /> The Ammo Channel, <i>AMMOTEST: 30 Caliber M2 (30-06) Armor Piercing Penetration Tests - Military Surplus Ammo</i> - <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hzYH8ap1TDo\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hzYH8ap1TDo</a>, Published June 21st, 2013, retrieved July 1st, 2023<br /> <br /> Tony DiGuilian, <i>USA 16&quot;/50 (40.6cm) Mark 7 - NavWeaps</i> - <a href=\"http://www.navweaps.com/Weapons/WNUS_16-50_mk7.php\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.navweaps.com/Weapons/WNUS_16-50_mk7.php</a>, Published July 17th, 2023, Retrieved July 17th, 2023.<br /> <br /> William H. Garzke, Jr. and Robert O. Dulin, Jr., <i>Battleships: United States Battleships, 1935 - 1992</i><br /> <br /> Woodworkweb, <i>Wood Strengths</i> - <a href=\"https://www.woodworkweb.com/woodwork-topics/wood/146-wood-strengths.html\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.woodworkweb.com/woodwork...strengths.html</a>, Publication date not listed, Retrieved July 17th, 2023.<br /> <br /> <br /> Updated July 18th 2023: Tuned the Durability factor of Granite.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My Project to convert Chronicles of Darkness to a data-driven <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd\" target=\"_blank\">Combined Arms Simulation: Gods of Iron</a><br /> My personal website, projects and games: <a href=\"https://puckerfactorgames.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://puckerfactorgames.com</a>","edited":{"userid":27707,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","user":"Jester-PFG","date":". 07-18-2023, 11:41 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","name":"Jester-PFG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27707_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-21T07:58:52-07:00","id":1518428,"title":"","content":"I can't seem to download the main doc<br /> <br /> Can you help out","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27385-cross-a-way","name":"Cross-a-way","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27385_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"106"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-21T13:30:30-07:00","id":1518449,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cross-a-way</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd?p=1518428#post1518428\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I can't seem to download the main doc<br /> <br /> Can you help out</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure, I forgot to tick the check box &quot;Allow Viewers to Download.&quot; It's been checked now. Let me know if it gives you any trouble.<br /> <br /> I try to update this when I can, so I've put the date on the front page so you'll be able to tell when a newer version is available. Stuff is fairly in flux, as this is a rough draft at the moment.<br /> <br /> The first time I tried to edit the first post it tripped spam detection on the forum, so I've avoided editing the first post. I do not know if the spam detection has been tuned since, but I want to err on the side of caution.<br /> <br /> The next update is due to include the mechanics on penetrating concrete fortifications, as well as something of an overhaul of vehicle armour.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My Project to convert Chronicles of Darkness to a data-driven <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd\" target=\"_blank\">Combined Arms Simulation: Gods of Iron</a><br /> My personal website, projects and games: <a href=\"https://puckerfactorgames.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://puckerfactorgames.com</a>","edited":{"userid":27707,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","user":"Jester-PFG","date":".  Edited for clarity. 07-21-2023, 04:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","name":"Jester-PFG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27707_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-22T10:34:20-07:00","id":1518543,"title":"","content":"Updated the main document today, making the following changes since last time:<ul><li>Added &quot;Structures, Emplacements, and Fortifications&quot;, which includes the content I typed up earlier, and more, including not only Durability of structures and fortifications, but also mechanics for things such as accumulated damage where repeated damage that doesn't overcome Durability can still erode the strength of the material, and mechanics for material degradation and failure modes. This is intended to be used to build castles, bunkers, pillboxes, and other targets for some of the upcoming weapons we're about to see in <i>Angels and Aces</i> including weapons such as the GBU-28 Laser Guided Bunker Busting Bomb, which I estimate may have a Damage + AP total north of 500, <i>not taking into account its explosive payload.</i></li> <li>Tune the Bow and Arrow to indicate that it is a Very Low Velocity weapon, and add a clarification for the Japanese Yumi.</li> <li>Double Barrelled Shotguns now behave like they did in 1st Edition.</li> <li>Add the Cobray Terminator as a joke gun upon special request. It is now a fully usable weapon in the Chronicles of Darkness and some of its particular quirks have been modelled in <i>Gods of Iron</i>.</li> <li>Add the KS-23 Shotgun and damage traits for the 23x75mmR shotgun shell.</li> <li>Tune characteristics for the 10-Gauge shotgun and 16-Gauge shotgun.</li> <li>Buff the 17th Century 4-gauge Blackpowder Blunderbuss.</li> <li>Add entries for the Nambu Type 14 Pistol and Koshikawa Arsenal Type 26 Revolver used by the Imperial Japanese Army, including special rules for their lack of reliability, as well as the Type 94 and Type 98 Shin Gunto in &quot;Ancient Weapons.&quot;</li> <li>Add mechanics for Performance Enhancing Drugs to the section on consumables.</li> </ul>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My Project to convert Chronicles of Darkness to a data-driven <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd\" target=\"_blank\">Combined Arms Simulation: Gods of Iron</a><br /> My personal website, projects and games: <a href=\"https://puckerfactorgames.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://puckerfactorgames.com</a>","edited":{"userid":27707,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","user":"Jester-PFG","date":". 07-22-2023, 01:39 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","name":"Jester-PFG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27707_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-24T20:29:06-07:00","id":1518738,"title":"","content":"Thanks man","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27385-cross-a-way","name":"Cross-a-way","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27385_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"106"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-04T16:28:56-07:00","id":1519799,"title":"","content":"Here's what I'm thinking for the next update, which will be about explosives. Because explosives are such important things on the modern battlefield, I wanted to take some time to give them some special attention.<br /> <br /> Because unlike GURPS, Chronicles of Darkness doesn't have its own explosives generation tables, I decided I would create some. There are some tables for first edition, but I decided I want to have a bit more detail.<br /> <br /> I've received a number of questions asking me why I go through such lengths to take an approach like this, and I'll explain a bit more: it's because it saves work in the long run. It's far easier for me to develop algorithms and formulas that I can easily automate and plug into a spreadsheet, which I can then apply stuff to. There could be many different types of shells and bombs in a country's national arsenal, and it's not easy for me to take a holistic approach to each individual one. Instead, if I can punch the numbers into a spreadsheet and have it spit out the numbers autonomously, or simply enter numbers into a spreadsheet. I make heavy use of automation when I game, so the economy of system complexity may be a tad bit different than if I was doing everything by hand. This process allows me to keep the amount of special rules and exceptions to a minimum and be able to get game stats relatively quickly off real-world metrics like charge mass and mmRHAe.<br /> <br /> First, there are a few thoughts I had in mind when I wanted to create these. The first thing was, I gained some newfound respect for the wounding system of Bashing, Lethal, and Aggravated Damage when I read a discussion on combat in Chronicles of Darkness by ArcaneArts, which discussed the different focus that Chronicles has that makes it different from games that use a traditional &quot;Hit Points&quot; based means of tracking damage, and how this is intended to allow for escalation of force to follow escalation of narrative; it makes sense that a game about narrative consequences would prioritize allowing players to do things like declare their intent before combat starts, and then vary the amount of force they plan to use to pursue their objectives in accordance with the stakes and the percieved consequences.<br /> <br /> This makes completely logical sense for a game with the focus that Chronicles of Darkness is intended to follow. While I wanted to follow convention in order to keep the game within its comfort zone, I felt that by the time players are turning to artillery, cruise missiles, bunker buster bombs, and carpet bombing, and expending possibly tons of ordnance, my players have already likely escalated the conflict a wee bit beyond the level of loading shotgun shells with silver shot.<br /> <br /> For this reason, I've decided to make a bit of a break from the usual, and I chose to take a multilevel approach to wounding that would be a tiny bit different. What I've chosen to do instead is to create a slightly different model of explosive damage for nonnuclear explosives, which would track blast overpressure and fragmentation damage separately.<br /> <br /> First, I'd like to reiterate: <i>this is being done for the purposes of gaming, <u>not</u> engineering.</i> The values are heavily approximated, compounding errors accrue, and I had to do some massaging to get them to fit within the Storyteller system.<br /> <br /> I decided to take a bit of a clean break from <i>Chronicles of Darkness: Hurt Locker</i>, and use different rules and mechanics for calculating explosive damage. These rules are no doubt going to be a wee bit more complicated, but are going to be easier to model things like the M830 HEAT-MP-T round fired by the M256 120mm Smoothbore Gun seen on the M1A2 Abrams, along with things like tomahawk cruise missiles carrying 1000-pound Joint Multi-Effect Warheads, GBU-24 Paveway IIIs, all the way up to the 1,900-pound Mark 13 High Capacity shell fire by the Mark 7 16&quot; Guns on the Iowa class battleships.<br /> <br /> I've tried to simplify things enough to keep this from becoming <i>Chronicles of Darkness: Phoenix Command,</i> but it's time to play with the big boys.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:22px\"><b><u>Establishing a Baseline</u></b></span><br /> <br /> As usual with everything I do, the first thing I want to attempt to do is establish a baseline, to determine the candid and realistic effects of the different weapons.<br /> <br /> First, what I decided to do was get some loose models, so I for input data I decided to base some of my estimates off the International Ammunition Technical Guidelines Formula for Ammunition Management.<br /> <br /> You can find a copy of the formulas here, and there is an automated web-based calculation of the Kingery-Bulmash Blast Parameter Calculator you can experiment with that does everything auto-magically here:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://unsaferguard.org/un-saferguard/kingery-bulmash\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://unsaferguard.org/themes/custom/iatg/img/UNSaferGuardLogo-2.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://unsaferguard.org/un-saferguard/kingery-bulmash\" target=\"_blank\"> Kingery-Bulmash Blast Parameter Calculator | International Ammunition Technical Guidelines </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://unsaferguard.org/un-saferguard/kingery-bulmash\" target=\"_blank\"> https://unsaferguard.org/un-saferguard/kingery-bulmash </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://data.unsaferguard.org/iatg/en/IATG-01.80-Formulae-ammunition-management-IATG-V.3.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://data.unsaferguard.org/iatg/en/IATG-01.80-Formulae-ammunition-management-IATG-V.3.pdf\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://data.unsaferguard.org/iatg/en/IATG-01.80-Formulae-ammunition-management-IATG-V.3.pdf\" target=\"_blank\"> https://data.unsaferguard.org/iatg/en/IATG-01.80-Formulae-ammunition-management-IATG-V.3.pdf </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> To condense the report, there are four primary ways that we can model people getting hurt from surface detonations. The first is blast overpressure caused by the explosive compound detonating into rapidly expanding gases which causes blast overpressure. The second is the mechanism of blast fragmentation, or fragments of material that are thrown about at high velocity by this action. The third, finally, are people being trapped within and crushed by collapsing structures blown down by the blast overpressure. Although it's quite plausible that a human being could survive a reflective overpressure of 5 pounds per square inch, the same pressure applied to the surface area of the side of a small residential house could quickly turn into many tons of force. The last is the thermal effects of the blast.<br /> <br /> We'll take the first two mechanisms for now, and what I chose to do is to track them separately. The effects of a fireball and structural collapses I won't track.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><b><u>Blast Overpressure</u></b></span><br /> <br /> In terms of blast overpressure, I went with the following estimates for reflective overpressure. A blast overpressure of 1 psi (pound per square inch) is usually enough to break a window. A blast overpressure of 3 psi is enough to cause injury to a human being, and cause serious damage to homes and wooden structures. Turn that up to 5 psi, and you're looking at serious injury to a person, and widespread destruction. At 10 psi, most structures that aren't reinforced won't be left standing and fatalities are likely going to be widespread. Double it once again to bring that up to 20psi, and the results are going to look like what you get in the aftermath of a hurricane along with fatalities approaching 100%.<br /> <br /> Blast overpressure drops off quickly in atmosphere, which means that damage inflicted due to overpressure should drop off quickly with range.<br /> <br /> ~1 psi is comparatively little and I'm using it to establish an upper limit to the radius of a blast. While this may not on its own cause injuries to most people, it can cause window glass to shatter, which could turn into shards. If windows only are worth Durability 1 Structure 1, this should establish a lower limit of 2 bashing damage at most. Beyond this, no damage should be incurred.<br /> <br /> &lt;5 psi should be considered to be an upper limit for bashing before it becomes lethal. A good estimate for this would be 6 automatic damage, and 6 damage dice.<br /> <br /> ~10 psi should be where Lethal Damage begins. 8 Lethal Damage is enough to fill the health tracks of the average person entirely and send them into bleedout, which we can take as &quot;widespread fatalities.&quot; This would be served well by 6 automatic damage, and 6 damage dice.<br /> <br /> &gt;20+ psi is the level at which lungs collapse or get perforated, and should be taken as Aggravated Damage. 8 Aggravated Damage should be considered to be instantly fatal to the average person, and very nearly fatal to anyone that lacks four Stamina dots and is at least Size 5. For the purposes of determining narrative effects (for the purposes of forensics and recovery), I would consider someone getting enough Aggravated Damage to fill their health track twice over to be on the level of total bodily disincorporation, but as a lower limit to this level going with 6 automatic damage and 6 damage dice which averages to 8.<br /> <br /> Looking at the math for blast overpressure using the Kingery-Bulmash equations and plotting them (rounded to the nearest metre) to the different reflective pressure thresholds, we get the following chart. We'll use the Kingery-Bulmash equations as a guide. Although these equations are intended to be used for a ground burst, given the low levels of accuracy involved with the system, it doesn't make much sense to redo this work for an airburst at optimal blast height.<br /> <br /> From here we can easily predict the following characteristics of the bursting charge of an M67 Hand Grenade without its fragmentation casing, which is 180g of Composition B explosive. Using the Kingery-Bulmash calculator, we obtain the following values for Reflected Pressure in kiloPascals, or 138kPa, 69kPa, and 38kPa coming in to play at 2.5m, 3.5m, and 5.5m respectively. This rounds to 3m, 4m, and 6m, and then drops off below 7kPa at 61m.<br /> <br /> This gives us an approximate baseline for the ranges at which the blast overpressure component of 180g of Composition B should inflict approximately 8 Aggravated, 8 Lethal, and 8 Bashing damage respectively for a ground burst, in the absence of its fragmentation casing. Rounding to the nearest whole number gives us 3, 4, and 6 metres respectively for each threshold.<br /> <br /> At one metre, the same explosive will give us a blast overpressure of close to 1700kPa, or approximately 250psi.<br /> <br /> For comparison, 1kg of TNT will produce the same pressures for the thresholds we're using as benchmarks at 4m, 6m, and 9m respectively, but also produce 8100kPa (1200psi) at a distance of 1m. Completing the chart below for TNT will provide us with some damage thresholds to work off of. TNT is ideal for establishing a baseline, as TNT is used as a benchmark for explosive equivalency, whether it be in kilograms or kilotonnes. If we keep on iterating, we can find that the blast overpressure of TNT reaches about 7kPa, or 1psi, at approximately 31m.<br /> <br /> I decided to complete a chart using these values for TNT. This chart can be used to indicate the expected values at which the different modes of damage should occur.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_description\">Code:</div> <pre class=\"bbcode_code notranslate\" style=\"max-height:calc(30 * 1.4rem + 2 * var(--bbcode-padding) + var(--h-scrollbar-allowance));\">Mass Type Peak Agv. Ltl. Bsh. Range &lt;=0.5 TNT 4250 2 3 4 24 1 TNT 8100 4 6 9 30 2 TNT 14698 5 8 11 39 4 TNT 24818 7 10 14 48 8 TNT 39493 9 12 17 61 16 TNT 60187 11 16 24 77 32 TNT 89384 14 19 27 97 64 TNT 130775 17 24 38 121 128 TNT 188391 22 31 47 154 256 TNT 264478 27 38 60 195 512 TNT 357979 34 48 76 245 1024 TNT 468623 43 62 95 309 2048 TNT 608491 54 78 120 390 4096 TNT 732065 69 98 150 498 8192 TNT 732538 87 123 190 619\u200b</pre> </div>These distances for the thresholds of damage have been derived using the Kingery-Bulmash equation for a hemispherical surface detonation and rounded to the nearest whole metre. Distances are in metres, peak explosive overpressure is in kiloPascals, mass is in kilograms. Aggravated damage is 20psi or ~139kPa, Lethal damage is 10psi or ~69kPa, and Bashing is reached at 5psi or ~21kPa, with maximum range dropping off to 1psi or ~7kPa.<br /> <br /> From this, what I've done is instead establish multiple threat radii we can then use as a base and develop the first leg of realistic explosives in Chronicles of Darkness, from 500g of TNT, all the way to 8 tonnes of TNT. Note that to keep the math easier for some other functions I doubled the values.<br /> <br /> We'll figure out damage in a bit, for now, let's got onto fragmentation.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><b><u>Blast Fragmentation</u></b></span><br /> <br /> Shortly before I wrote this, I tried using the Gurney Equations to calculate the initial velocity of fragments for different ratios of explosive to uniform casing, then attempted to use that to calculate trajectories to plot the course that the fragments would take so I could develop an estimate of what fragments would land where at what velocity, and then remembered my mantra of <i>this is a game, not engineering.</i><br /> <br /> Perhaps that's not such a great approach.<br /> <br /> Fortunately for us, the IATG also has a relatively simple Explosion Danger Area Calculator that delivers an extremely conservative estimate for an approximate distance outside of which not more than one fragment could be expected to fly when destroying ammunition by simply detonating it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://unsaferguard.org/un-saferguard/explosion-danger-area\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://unsaferguard.org/themes/custom/iatg/img/UNSaferGuardLogo-2.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://unsaferguard.org/un-saferguard/explosion-danger-area\" target=\"_blank\"> Explosion Danger Area Calculator | International Ammunition Technical Guidelines </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://unsaferguard.org/un-saferguard/explosion-danger-area\" target=\"_blank\"> https://unsaferguard.org/un-saferguard/explosion-danger-area </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> As luck would have it the math is pretty basic, however there are three different formulas for three different situations: for fragmenting munitions when the public is or isn't allowed access to the demolition area, and for the bare exposed explosive only. Although this is misusing the formula, I decided that using the formula for the bare explosive actuallay gave results closer to what I was hoping for for the lower end of things, especially for things like grenades and mortar shells.<br /> <br /> This gives us the following chart:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_description\">Code:</div> <pre class=\"bbcode_code notranslate\" style=\"max-height:calc(30 * 1.4rem + 2 * var(--bbcode-padding) + var(--h-scrollbar-allowance));\">AUW Range Max Primary Secondary 0.25 82 4 21 41 0.5 103 5 26 52 1 130 6 33 65 2 164 7 41 82 4 206 8 52 103 8 260 9 65 130 16 328 10 82 164 32 413 11 103 207 64 520 12 130 260 128 655 13 164 328 256 825 14 206 413 512 1040 15 260 520 1024 1310 16 328 655 2048 1651 17 413 826 4096 2080 18 520 1040 8192 2621 19 655 1311\u200b</pre> </div>Where AUW stands for &quot;All-Up Weight&quot; including the packing and palletization, Ranges, including Primary and Secondary Ranges are measured in metres. Max is the maximum damage that fragmentation effects should inflict, we'll discuss this in greater detail in a bit. To obtain the damage value, what I did was take the all-up weight in kilograms, take a binary logarithm, and add 6.<br /> <br /> As luck has it, this works out quite well for a rough estimate. If we were to take this chart and apply it to an M67 Grenade, which weighs about 400g all in, the M67 has a published injury radius of 15m, but can certainly toss fragments out to a quarter of a kilometre, although of course they could be pretty well dispersed by then. If we round up to 500g and apply the formula, we end up with something around half that, which could be considered close enough for government work.<br /> <br /> From this we can now establish an approximate upper limit on how far damaging fragments or automatic damage should occur in game, scaled relative to the size of a bomb or explosive.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:24px\"><b><u>Putting it all together: Damage</u></b></span><br /> <br /> Here's the fun part. Now that we've figured out the area of effect and the radius of the blasts of the bombs, now let's take a few minutes to figure out what kind of damage they can do.<br /> <br /> Unfortunately, I don't have any scientific way of translating real world data to game units, so what I've gone with is a slightly holistic approach that I determined based on the time honoured process of adjusting the numbers using coefficients and adjustment factors until I come up with something that looks like it would fit into the game's existing stats until something comes out looking like the stats that I wanted it to.<br /> <br /> A short sidenote I'd like to take is that <i>New World of Darkness: Armory, 1st Edition</i> had an interesting rule that allowed explosive damage to scale with the size of the objects, to represent objects of greater size taking more damage from reflected blast pressure and increased surface area that fragmentation could affect. <i>Hunter: The Vigil, 1st Edition</i> also included mechanics for fragmenting objects. We're going to be bringing both of those back in a slighty modified way to account for hardened armoured vehicles which can withstand a near miss from even 1000-pound bombs, but for now we'll focus simply on the raw damage output.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><b><u>Damage Due To Blast Pressure</u></b></span><br /> <br /> What I've done so far is apply the function of <b>log(Peak Pressure/4200,1.125)+6</b> to the Peak Pressure of the explosion determined using the Kingery-Bulmash equations, and used that to calculate the maximum damage that the explosion would cause at the point at which its blast pressure is the greatest. This establishes a fourth bracket for the explosive damage, which I figure should be taken as the maximum damage that the explosive should do at its origin. I then split up the ranges using a simple linear rule for simplicity.<br /> <br /> From there, we can produce the following results:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_description\">Code:</div> <pre class=\"bbcode_code notranslate\" style=\"max-height:calc(30 * 1.4rem + 2 * var(--bbcode-padding) + var(--h-scrollbar-allowance));\">Mass Type kPa Base 1/4 Force 1/2 Force 3/4 Force 20p Force &lt;=0.5 TNT 4250 6 1 6 1 6 2 6 2 6 1 TNT 8100 12 1 10 2 9 3 7 4 6 2 TNT 14698 17 1 14 3 10 4 8 5 6 4 TNT 24818 21 2 16 4 12 5 10 7 6 8 TNT 39493 25 2 20 5 14 7 10 9 6 16 TNT 60187 29 3 22 6 16 8 12 11 6 32 TNT 89384 32 4 24 7 19 11 11 14 6 64 TNT 130775 35 4 28 9 19 13 12 17 6 128 TNT 188391 38 6 29 11 22 17 13 22 6 256 TNT 264478 41 7 31 14 22 20 15 27 6 512 TNT 357979 44 9 33 17 25 26 14 34 6 1024 TNT 468623 46 11 35 22 25 32 16 43 6 2048 TNT 608491 48 14 37 27 27 41 16 54 6 4096 TNT 732065 50 17 39 35 27 52 16 69 6 8192 TNT 732538 50 22 38 44 27 65 17 87 6\u200b</pre> </div>Let's take two worked examples of play and derive the stats for approximately one pound of TNT which is being set off with no fragmentation. First, we round to the closest number on the chart which gives us 500g of TNT. This gives us a base of 6 Force, Aggravated.<br /> <br /> At 2m, and all ranges underneath, it will do Force 6A: six automatic Aggravated Damage, and six damage dice rolled. Between 2-3m, it will do Force 6L: six automatic Lethal Damage, and six damage dice rolled. At 3-4m, it will do Force 6B: six automatic Bashing Damage, and six damage dice rolled. Between 4m and 24m, this will gradually taper off to Force 1B at 24m. Beyond 24m, the blast itself in the absence of any fragmentation does no damage at all. A simple linear function can take care of this part, so I won't go into detail there.<br /> <br /> Our second worked example will be for a Mark 84 General Purpose Bomb, a freefall bomb with approximately 1000 pounds of Tritonal filling. We could go through the exercise of multiplying all the distance values by a conversion factor to re-run the numbers for Tritonal 80-20 to TNT, but in the interest of expediency, we'll simply use the numbers for 512kg of TNT and call it close enough.<br /> <br /> We look at the numbers and see that when the Mark 84 detonates, the blast pressure will deal Force 44A on everything within a 9m radius of where it detonated, in the form of 44 Automatic Damage and 44 Damage Dice. This drops to Force 25A between 9-17m, Force 14A between 17-26m, and then finally drops off to Force 6A between 26-34m. Between 34-48m, this becomes Force 6L, 48-76m becomes Force 6B, and then tapers off to Force 1B over the course of 76-245m. Again, this is in the absence of any metal casing, so we'll apply fragmentation damage later.<br /> <br /> Because the order of Bashing damage and Lethal damage counts and Bashing damage applied prior to Lethal damage is &quot;pushed off&quot; the health track, rather than upgraded, when dealing with explosives order of precedence applies and blast pressure damage is applied first, so it can be pushed off, rather than upgrade once fragmentation enters the fray.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><b><u>Damage due to Blast Fragmentation</u></b></span><br /> <br /> What I've decided to do with blast fragmentation is a bit simpler. The full fragmentation damage is done, in Force, at a quarter of the range, half fragmentation damage, again in Force, is done at half, and a quarter of the fragmentation damage is done between, rounding to the nearest whole number.<br /> <br /> The All-Up Weight is used to calculate damage due to blast fragmentation, up to a limit of twice the mass of the explosive.<br /> <br /> This is thankfully, a little simpler. Let's do another worked example again.<br /> <br /> Let's say that Scratch the Changeling wants to make a pipebomb out of a pound of TNT. He goes and gets the TNT, and wraps it all manner of nasty metal stuff that he could expect to turn into a lethal cloud of shards when the TNT detonates. By the end of it, Scratch's IED consists of 500g of TNT, and 1kg of fragmentation casing and weighs in at 1.5kg. We round this to 2kg for determining the All-Up-Weight. We consult the chart and see that that gives us that it's a Force 7 Weapon, with a primary blast radius of 0-41m, a secondary blast radius of 41-82m, and a tertiary blast radius of 82-164m. If Scratch were to use Cold Iron to produce the bomb casing, this becomes Aggravated when used against Fey creatures. This is partially why I preferred to scale the number, rather than scale the damage type.<br /> <br /> In terms of fragmentation damage, this gives us damage of Force 7L at 0-41m, Force 3L at 41-82m, and Force 1L at 82m. This is applied on the same turn as the detonation, but after the explosive overpressure damage is applied.<br /> <br /> Now, for our other worked example, the Mark 84 Bomb. The Mark 84 bomb is only fifty percent explosive by mass, and the entire bomb weighs close to two thousand pounds. Rounded to the nearest value on the charts, we can take the value for AUW as 1024kg. The base value for its fragmentation damage will be 16, and have a range of 1310. So this means that everything within a 0-328m radius of the detonation will take a Force 16L attack, everything within a 328-655m radius of the detonation will take a Force 8L attack, and everything within a 655-1310m radius will take a Force 4L attack. This again stacks with the damage done by the air blast overpressure, Order of Precedence applying.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><b><u>Other Types of Explosives</u></b></span><br /> <br /> The wonderful world of things that go bang aren't limited to TNT. Explosives are just as varied as the people who deploy them, and there are explosives like RDX, HMX, and other blends like Amatol, Tritonite, and more.<br /> <br /> We could simply recalculate all the values, but it's probably just easier to multiply them by a conversion factor and then get an idea of TNT equivalency instead. For instance, the brisance of Black Powder is very low and the relative effectiveness factor could be used to count it as only half the effectiveness of TNT by weight, and primary explosives like Mercury (II) Fulminate might only be a third. Something like RDX (Hexogen/Trinitrotriazinane) might count for one and a half. This way, we don't need to worry about doing a lot of complicated math in-game but still can capture the nuance to a certain extent.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><b><u>To close out...</u></b></span><br /> <br /> This was a big one, so thanks for staying with me on this. I'll be gradually phasing these in across <i>Gods of Iron</i>, and in the near future I'll be implementing some other mechanics for other effects such as HEAT, HESH, Shaped Charges, and the like. This changes gameplay in a great big way because it makes explosions a whole lot deadlier and more realistic, at the cost of some complexity.<br /> <br /> There are a few hacks that you can use with these rules, such as not allowing blast overpressure and fragmentation damage to stack, which brings the performance of hand grenades and sticks of dynamite more in line with the values in Hurt Locker.<br /> <br /> For now though, these guidelines are going to be powering some of the estimates I'll be using for things such as 155mm Howitzer shells, bombs, vehicle-mounted weaponry, artillery, and missiles and paving the way for the larger scale weaponry in <i>Angels and Aces</i> and <i>Seas of Steel</i> for use not just on the tactical scale, but also on the operational and strategic level.<br /> <br /> As always, ideas that may assist with improving this are certainly welcome.<br /> \u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My Project to convert Chronicles of Darkness to a data-driven <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd\" target=\"_blank\">Combined Arms Simulation: Gods of Iron</a><br /> My personal website, projects and games: <a href=\"https://puckerfactorgames.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://puckerfactorgames.com</a>","edited":{"userid":27707,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","user":"Jester-PFG","date":". 08-04-2023, 07:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","name":"Jester-PFG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27707_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-07T19:57:38-07:00","id":1520074,"title":"","content":"My previous post got flagged for spam about two weeks ago and hasn't cleared queue yet, so I'm going to assume it's gotten lost and repost it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Updated the main document today, making the following changes since last time:<ul><li>Added &quot;Structures, Emplacements, and Fortifications&quot;, which includes the content I typed up earlier, and more, including not only Durability of structures and fortifications, but also mechanics for things such as accumulated damage where repeated damage that doesn't overcome Durability can still erode the strength of the material, and mechanics for material degradation and failure modes. This is intended to be used to build castles, bunkers, pillboxes, and other targets for some of the upcoming weapons we're about to see in <i>Angels and Aces</i> including weapons such as the GBU-28 Laser Guided Bunker Busting Bomb, which I estimate may have a Damage + AP total north of 500, <i>not taking into account its explosive payload.</i></li> <li>Tune the Bow and Arrow to indicate that it is a Very Low Velocity weapon, and add a clarification for the Japanese Yumi.</li> <li>Double Barrelled Shotguns now behave like they did in 1st Edition.</li> <li>Add the Cobray Terminator as a joke gun upon special request. It is now a fully usable weapon in the Chronicles of Darkness and some of its particular quirks have been modelled in <i>Gods of Iron</i>.</li> <li>Add the KS-23 Shotgun and damage traits for the 23x75mmR shotgun shell.</li> <li>Tune characteristics for the 10-Gauge shotgun and 16-Gauge shotgun.</li> <li>Buff the 17th Century 4-gauge Blackpowder Blunderbuss.</li> <li>Add entries for the Nambu Type 14 Pistol and Koshikawa Arsenal Type 26 Revolver used by the Imperial Japanese Army, including special rules for their lack of reliability, as well as the Type 94 and Type 98 Shin Gunto in &quot;Ancient Weapons.&quot;</li> <li>Add mechanics for Performance Enhancing Drugs to the section on consumables.</li> </ul>\u200b </div> </div> </div>Current Updates<ul><li>Tune the M3 Bradley's Turret Traverse Speed.</li> <li>Add the Centurion Mk. III, along with the Sho't Kal, and Sho't Kal Dalet variants.</li> <li>Add the Royal Ordnance L7 105mm Gun and its ammunition, including M393A2 HESH rounds with full mechanics, using the new explosive mechanics.</li> <li>Add the Royal Ordnance 84mm QF 20-Pounder Gun and its ammunitio, again using the new explosive mechanics.</li> </ul>Over the weekend I decided to add another extremely famous and iconic tank on request from a friend, the old British Centurion. I've added fully detailed entries for the Royal Ordnance 84mm Quick-Firing 20-Pounder Gun, and I added the missing entry for the Royal Ordnance 105mm L7 Rifled Gun, which saw service in the United States as the M68 and M68A1 Rifled Gun. This adds the missing entry for the M60A3(TTS)'s main gun (how embarrassing I forgot it!), and I've included entries for several different shells for the QF-20 and the L7 Gun, including the M900 105mm APFSDS fired by the M1 Abrams and the M393A2 HESH round. Early mechanics for the HESH rounds are being done with this shell being used as a reference example.<br /> <br /> Although I haven't finished the rules for the explosives update, they're undergoing evaluation right now and in anticipation of getting them implemented, both the L7 Gun and the QF-20's different shells have been statted up using the new rules for deriving explosive damage and blast radius using the Kingery-Bulmash equations and other guidelines established under the International Ammunition Technical Guidelines.<br /> <br /> I'm very pleased to say that the M393A2 High Explosive Squash Head shell for the L7 Gun is performing as expected. I wanted to wait until I had finished the explosive rules draft before I did the HESH rounds, and what I've come up with is to use the primary blast force of the explosive carried by the shell, notwithstanding the increase due to size, to determine if the shell puts enough energy into the armour to cause it to spall. This can then create an outburst on the opposite side of the cover, which uses the stats of a fragmentation burst from its parent weapon. I relied on War Thunder's benchmark of the M393A2 HESH round being able to penetrate 130mm of Rolled Homogenous Armour at any range and at any angle barring a ricochet. The stats given for 4kg of TNT or equivalent place the base damage at Force 21, which will roll to an average of 27 damage, which is almost exactly on the tick of 140mm, or just 10mm off the mark. Not bad for my estimates!<br /> <br /> Also with the proposed rules, the new tables for the GBU-28 Laser Guided Bunker Busting Bomb would have the following stats. This is a proposal for the stats for the GBU-28, which I'm going to put into the main branch later.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Circular Error Probable:</b> &lt;2m, limited by the accuracy of the targeting laser. It's possible for laser guided bombs to be sent down ventilation shafts, chimneys, and even sent through windows, so 2m is quite a lot.<br /> <b>Kinetic Damage:</b> 3d10+79 AP 575, when travelling at terminal velocity<br /> <b>Air Blast Force, Aggravated:</b> Force 41 at 0-7m, Force 31 at 8-14m, Force 15 at 15-20m, Force 6 at 21-27m<br /> <b>Air Blast Force, Lethal:</b> Force 6 at 28-38m<br /> <b>Air Blast Force, Bashing:</b> Force 6 at 39-60m, tapering off to Force 1B at 195m at a rate of 1 Force lost, per every 22m beyond 60m.<br /> <b>Fragmentation Force:</b> Force 15 Lethal at 0-260m, Force 7 Lethal at 261-520m, and Force 3 Lethal at 521-1040m. </div> </div> </div>By the tables, this means that the GBU-28 has a 50% chance of successfully penetrating cover rated Durability 679, or the in-game equivalent of 28 feet of high-strength, reinforced concrete[1]. Once it does, on average it should cause a total of 54 damage in explosive damage once the dice have been rolled, cumulative with the damage done via my rules for explosive order of precedence on the structure. Because bunkers and hardened underground bases aren't big open spaces, one way to simulate a contained explosion would be to raise the primary blast radius to the extents of the structure unless the structure is big enough that it can contain several times the entirety of the blast volume, which would also raise the damage done to the structure accordingly to scale alongside it.<br /> <br /> It's reasonable to assume that this would cause the Earthquake tilt for a couple turns however I don't have mechanics for that quite yet.<br /> <br /> This is a very quick and dirty rule, but I think if I was to find a quick and dirty &quot;rule of thumb&quot; to determine damage done by collapsing structures, in the absence of the GM's fiat, I would simply just go with the rule of &quot;apply the greater of either half the structure's size, or its durability as automatic bashing damage to the occupants.&quot;<br /> <br /> <b>Footnotes</b><br /> [1] This has been estimated. During testing, an inert GBU-28 casing made from an old howitzer barrel was tested and punched right through 22 feet of high-strength, steel-reinforced concrete and had enough leftover energy to travel on its merry way for another two and a half kilometres.<br /> <br /> \u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My Project to convert Chronicles of Darkness to a data-driven <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd\" target=\"_blank\">Combined Arms Simulation: Gods of Iron</a><br /> My personal website, projects and games: <a href=\"https://puckerfactorgames.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://puckerfactorgames.com</a>","edited":{"userid":27707,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","user":"Jester-PFG","date":".  Edited to add a brief summary of the changes made in point form. 08-08-2023, 09:15 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","name":"Jester-PFG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27707_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-31T15:00:16-07:00","id":1521785,"title":"","content":"Hi everybody,<br /> <br /> I haven't made a lot of progress so far in the main branch, but I wanted to provide some updates<br /> <br /> First up, I have a very embarassing correction to make: the previous stats for fortifications are incorrect; I actually calculated them out as equivalency in mmRHAe, not Durability, so I've amended that and fixed the PDF. My embarrassment! I'll go back and change it to Durability at a later time to simplify the calculations later. This means I'll redo the calculations for the GBU-28, fortunately, that shouldn't be too difficult. Big whoopsie on my part!<br /> <br /> I'm taking a moment to stray from the explosives update to work on the Nightvision Optical Devices update. Virtually all tanks later than Cold War era tanks have either night and/or thermal imaging, analogue or digital fire control systems, so without them, many of the Cold War era tanks are underperforming. I've decided to cover nightvision optical devices and thermal imaging devices generation-by-generation, and in order to do this I've been writing mechanics for low-light engagements and night fighting, including detailed mechanics for visibility states and spotting and identification at six different stages of lighting, along with mechanics for representing four different generations of night vision optical devices that correspond to the real-world generations, including the new Dutch 4G standard (Generation IV isn't quite a thing in real life yet and it's more like a Generation III-Plus, but I decided to add it anyways)<br /> <br /> Rather than spell out each individual model specifically, I'm including mechanics for you to create your own configurations, ranging from Single Tube Biocular configurations, to Monocle, to Dual-Tube Binocular, all the way to Multi-Tube Panoramic arrangements and at different generations, allowing you to represent many different combinations, configurations, and technology levels of different night vision optical devices. This in short will allow your troupe to be able to recreate iconic Night Vision Optical devices ranging from the primitive AN/PVS-1 Starlight Scope of Vietnam War era fame, to the modern L3 Harris Ground Panoramic Night Vision Goggles featured in movies like <i>Zero Dark Thirty</i> and <i>Sicario: Day of the Soldato.</i><br /> <br /> The Night Fighting update will include mechanics and detailed examples of play for fighting in low light engagements, greatly expanding upon the tactical possibilities offered within the scope of a tabletop gaming system, with a total of six different visibility states. It will work in harmony with the mechanics for armoured fighting vehicles, which often make use of much more sophisticated thermal and night vision sighting. I hope to have this done next update.<br /> <br /> So, hopefully over the course of the next bunch of updates I'd like to:<ul><li>Tune some of the damage tables for APFSDS rounds.<br /> <b>Progress: </b>Ongoing, currently I just reverted some of the rules for the modern APFSDS rounds which now see modern APFSDS do damage as per a full calibre round. The net penetration values have remained the same. This is due to the lateral that they have on the target, and I was double-dipping by reducing their fragmentation value. This has been corrected so far for the Royal Ordnance L7 and M68 105mm Tank Gun.</li> <li>Finish the Night Fighting Update and the optics.<br /> <b>Progress:</b> Most of the rules have already been written for this and I'm slowly moving them in and writing the fluff.</li> <li>Finish the fire control system rules for vehicles<br /> <b>Progress: </b>Not started yet, modern AFVs are currently underperforming.</li> <li>Finish the Explosives Update and remove all the legacy placeholder values for explosives, replacing them with the next generation explosives tables.</li> <li><b>Progress:</b> Most of the underlying math and calculations for this has been done, I need to write the rules, as well as update the rules of what happens when a cannon shell penetrates into and detonates <i>inside</i> a monster for the next-generation explosive mechanics.</li> <li>Convert the values for fortifications from mmRHAe back into Durability<br /> <b>Progress:</b> Haven't started yet but this should be a fairly simple job to do in a spreadsheet.</li> <li>Finish the Turn Radius values for vehicles.<br /> <b>Progress:</b> Currently haven't started this yet.</li> <li>Update the examples of play accordingly, including the M103's example that I said I would.<br /> <b>Progress: </b>Queued, I will finish this once I have the other prerequisite work done.</li> </ul>In the meantime, I've gotten a few suggestions that I've sought out, and I'll sum up some thoughts.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b><u>Proposed Ideas and Feedback</u></b></span><br /> <br /> I've gotten some feedback off of the board from some other people who play Chronicles and some of the most recurring asks and requests I've got were not for new tanks and new AFVs, but rather for a few other things, the first three of which didn't occur to me:<br /> <br /> <b><u>Simplified Armoured Fighting Vehicle Rules and Tables</u></b><br /> <br /> Currently, a number of people have told me that the way I do tank mechanics is a little bit complicated for their comfort, and most players and GMs don't have their characters slug it out in tanks the way some of my tables tend to. It appears that many players who play Chronicles of Darkness don't intend to use armoured fighting vehicles as more than targets for or background setpieces for encounters with big, powerful monsters or high powered player characters, so writing detailed tables for them is a bit of overkill. While I felt that they were quite brief, lightweight, and simple, it appears that I have a very different definition of what is &quot;crunchy&quot; compared to most players.<br /> <br /> At the moment, to field a somewhat modern tank that would be in service today, players reading Gods of Iron as of this current update would need to first decide if they want it to be a T-72B3 obr.2016 or T-72B, Sho't Kal Dalet, M60A3-TTS, T-90M, Leopard 2A4 or 2A7V, or an M1A2 Abrams SEPv4, look up the particular gun it uses, then determine the number and loadout of the shells and shell type, add the secondary weapons, and then use the different rules particular for each of the different vehicles. For most players that just want &quot;a tank&quot; to work as either a target for their player characters to take on, or an environmental hazard for them to avoid and aren't too picky about what it's going to be, this presents quite a large number of options which most players probably aren't going to need or want. While I encourage players and GMs to challenge themselves to maintain tactical correctness, I understand that not everyone will have the experience, knowledge, or desire to do this.<br /> <br /> Because of this, I'm considering adding another section which will have some simplified rules, such as for instance, instead of having separate tables for the Royal Ordnance L30A1, Rheinmetall Rh-120 L/55A1, and the Spetstekhnika Design Bureau 2A46-5 Guns (all of those are modern tank guns in use today), I would consider them all under the same &quot;Generic&quot; entry, and simply include entries for the following under the simplified rules which would be there to supplant, but <i>not</i> to replace the &quot;full&quot; rules.<ul><li>Generic First World War Tank Gun</li> <li>Generic Second World War Era Medium Tank Gun</li> <li>Generic Second World War Era Heavy Tank Gun</li> <li>Generic Cold War Era Light Tank Gun</li> <li>Generic Cold War Era Heavy Tank Gun</li> <li>Generic Modern Tank Gun</li> </ul>I would likely give them three ammunition types for each: Armour Piercing, High Explosive, and Antipersonnel, and probably some simplified rules. Again, I'd be doing this with the idea that the simplified or generic rules would be there for GMs that aren't intending to capture a lot of tactical vehicle combined arms combat and would like an encounter setpiece, a target, or an environmental hazard without needing to introduce much crunch.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Suggestions and GM'ing Hints for Integrating AFVs and Military Technology with Supernatural Elements</u></b><br /> <br /> With only a few exceptions I've been avoiding spelling out explicitly how the supernatural interacts with military technology that's not in the core rulebook. The reason why I've done this is because that the limitations your group puts on how supernatural talents that don't have their limits spelled out interact with elements that weren't ever intended to be in the game are going to greatly influence how it plays, and what &quot;genre&quot; you choose to make your chronicle.<br /> <br /> For instance, on one hand if you decide that <i>&quot;the limits on this power aren't established, therefore this power has no limits&quot;</i> and decide to embrace that as a core aspect of gameplay, this can lead to a much more heroic game. If you decide your group's Changeling Wizened who has the Gremlin kith and bought the Ogre seeming Benefit for Tatterdemalion's Workshop can singlehandedly churn out more missiles than US Air Force's entire peacetime stockpile from a few stacks of paper airplanes and a bunch of party poppers in an instant, because <i>&quot;the book doesn't say I can't!&quot;</i> it could make for a silly, messy, cartoony, over-the-top power fantasy that a lot of people could find very appealing.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, if you decide to set some ground rules such as establishing hard limits if they aren't stated and establishing limits on how many supernatural powers can be &quot;stacked&quot; to gain the cumulative sum of their benefits, this can make for a much grittier, more tactical game, where despite your characters' fantastic abilities, offensives need to be planned and orchestrated at multiple levels, resources, fuel and ammunition need to be conserved and managed, and each metre of land won is paid for dearly in blood, sweat, and tears. Admittedly, this is more to my speed and more to my intentions when I wrote this, but I know that not every group will want to play it this way.<br /> <br /> I don't view it as my job to tell you which of those extremes or wherever inbetween your troupe should be playing, since I very firmly believe that what genre your troupe wants to run is a personal choice that should be a part of the discussion and consensus that players and GM should arrive at before anybody so much as puts a pencil onto their charactersheet. However, I think that it might be a great idea for me to spell out some <i>possibilities</i> as to how your troupe can tailor your game, which could help get the conversation started.<br /> <br /> <b><u>More Merits and Character Customization Options</u></b><br /> <br /> One of the requests I got which was super well thought out was a request for more character customization options that would work in harmony with some of the newer content I've been working with, but also most importantly, work without it in &quot;normal&quot; (as in, non-Combined Arms) games, similar to how Hurt Locker has numerous options, fighting styles, and supernatural merits which can go a long way to making a character feel different, unique, and special.<br /> <br /> I don't want to overwrite or duplicate the work of any of the mechanics in Hurt Locker, but I'm going to start collecting ideas to make this happen. Ideally, I think I ought to keep them related to technology and security to keep some clear delineation.<br /> <br /> <b><u>More Civilian Vehicles</u></b><br /> <br /> A few people have requested a number of iconic vehicles, (and yes, the 1967 Chevrolet Impala 4-Door Hard Top was on that list!) that could be done with the enhanced rules and added realism seen in Gods of Iron, so I've collected a wishlist of civilian vehicles, and I'd like to write mechanics for motorcycles, which would have their traits derived from their operator's Dexterity and Drive dots up to a &quot;reference limit&quot;. This has been on the wishlist for a while, but it's been a lower priority. However hearing other people requesting it has made me thought that perhaps I should make it a higher priority item.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> To close out, as always, feedback, suggestions, recommendations and corrections that may help to improve this document are most welcome.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My Project to convert Chronicles of Darkness to a data-driven <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd\" target=\"_blank\">Combined Arms Simulation: Gods of Iron</a><br /> My personal website, projects and games: <a href=\"https://puckerfactorgames.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://puckerfactorgames.com</a>","edited":{"userid":27707,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","user":"Jester-PFG","date":".  Minor additions, some grammatical cleanup and clarification. 08-31-2023, 07:23 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","name":"Jester-PFG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27707_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-31T22:18:03-07:00","id":1521804,"title":"","content":"I didn't find a reference on either page, so I need to ask if anyone has actually referred you over to <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/3292/World-of-Darkness-Armory?src=also_purchased\" target=\"_blank\">Armory</a> and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/56496/World-of-Darkness-Dogs-of-War\" target=\"_blank\">Dogs of War</a>. Like, kudos if you enjoy crunchier mechanics for your passions, but if you're trying to make something the conventional Chronicles player will use, it might help to know how Chronicles has already approached military campaigns.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-01T14:51:18-07:00","id":1521837,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd?p=1521804#post1521804\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I didn't find a reference on either page, so I need to ask if anyone has actually referred you over to <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/3292/World-of-Darkness-Armory?src=also_purchased\" target=\"_blank\">Armory</a> and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/56496/World-of-Darkness-Dogs-of-War\" target=\"_blank\">Dogs of War</a>. Like, kudos if you enjoy crunchier mechanics for your passions, but if you're trying to make something the conventional Chronicles player will use, it might help to know how Chronicles has already approached military campaigns.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hey that's a great point. I appreciate the heads-up and it's a good thing you mentioned them in order to minimize duplication of effort. I do have the books but sometimes it's easy for me to get carried away and end up reinventing the wheel. I think in that case then I ought to scale back working on new merits, since there's still a lot of those in both <i>Armoury 1st Edition,</i> <i>Armoury: Reloaded 1st Edition</i>, <i>Dogs of War</i> that won't take anybody much effort to bring forward, and <i>Hurt Locker</i> has merits by the heap. I'll try to keep things topical to the detailed stuff - the stuff that won't be found anywhere else - in the meantime and focus on getting that Explosives update done faster, thanks! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My Project to convert Chronicles of Darkness to a data-driven <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd\" target=\"_blank\">Combined Arms Simulation: Gods of Iron</a><br /> My personal website, projects and games: <a href=\"https://puckerfactorgames.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://puckerfactorgames.com</a>","edited":{"userid":27707,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","user":"Jester-PFG","date":". 09-01-2023, 05:56 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","name":"Jester-PFG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27707_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-02T19:30:35-07:00","id":1521936,"title":"","content":"I wanted to follow up a little bit to explain some of the differences between <i>Gods of Iron</i> and <i>Dogs of War</i> in a few different areas.<br /> <br /> I want to stress, I <i>don't</i> want this to be taken as the idea that one is better than the other, I just want to establish where the differences between them are architecturally at a high level and what my intentions are. <i>Dogs of War</i>, <i>Armory</i>, and what I do seem to be adjacent, but very different, and I wanted to compare-and-contrast what they do and how they might be able to contribute value in different areas to a game.<br /> <br /> So I thought I'd break down my thoughts on both:<br /> <br /> <b><u>Backgrounds, Settings, and Contexts</u></b><br /> <br /> <b>Dogs of War</b> includes a lot of modern day events and modern conflicts, and casts them in a supernatural/occult light. It provides suggestions, NPCs, and dramatic figures and both conventional and unconventional militaries, provides supporting characters, reference examples of stock characters and antagonists the GM could insert into any campaign or adjust to put into an existing one, tools and suggestions for building military characters and scope for backstories. It provides descriptions of conflict zones and goes into detail about the skills that soldiers tend to have and how they tend to get them, and even suggests events that a character could have gone through during basic training. <i>Dogs of War</i> describes some general situations in military life, such as a character getting hazed by a bully during basic training, all the way to having to deal with the stress and horror of active war zones.<br /> <br /> <b>Gods of Iron</b> focuses on mostly hard factors, and assumes that your GM and players will already know what would be the correct grades of ammunition in service at the appropriate time period and location; for instance, the game will draw the distinction between a T-72A Ural, a T-72B, and a T-72B3 obr.2016, which despite being the same tank chassis, all use different amendments of the 2A46 Tank Gun, which in turn enables different ammunition types to be used, and it is the GM's responsibility to set the technology capabilities accordingly. As a result, it offers no historical context into the setting as to do so would be redundant given the target audience. No NPCs or dramatis personae are provided, supernatural or otherwise.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Vehicles</u></b><br /> <br /> <b>Armoury</b> assumes that you are only ever going to use the armoured fighting vehicles in it for the purposes of a military response to a large and/or powerful monster, and are more done from the standpoint that armoured fighting vehicles are just there to serve as targets or as environmental hazards for your player characters. This approach is intended to be simple and fast to avoid bogging down the game in superfluous detail. <i>Armoury </i>takes a &quot;less is more&quot; approach, to avoid burdening the GM with too many choices.<br /> <br /> <b>Gods of Iron</b> provides a brief amount of history on specific units detailing their countries of origin, a bit of their history, and rules specific to each one. Vehicles are created using reference data to translate performance metrics and hard factors obtained from wartime testing or open source intelligence translated into the game via a system of algorithms. <i>Gods of Iron</i> assumes that game balance will be achieved by using vehicles of comparable era and capability with other vehicles of comparable era and capability. Currently the game has more than seven different main battle tanks which are still in service today, most of which have different sub variants and configurations. The depictions are intended to be candid; technological disparities are expected to play out similarly as to they do in the real world, without regard to game balance.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Large Engagements</u></b><br /> <br /> <b>Dogs of War</b> attempts to <i>avoid</i> large engagements, by allowing units to boil down encounters between large units to a series of dice rolls without needing a map. Assets such as firepower and unit training and competency are boiled down to a series of attribute scores ranked on a scale of 1 to 10, and advantages such as equipment and assets and defence to a series of attribute scores ranked on a scale of 1 to 5 to allow encounters between big groups to be represented through the use of dice rolls. This can then be used to drive framing for what the player characters are doing.<br /> <br /> <b>Gods of Iron</b>'s vehicle mechanics assumes your troupe will be playing out full scale engagements with combined arms warfare at true-to-scale or near-true-to-scale tactical maps, taking into account not only positioning but also facing and angle-of-impact, including simplified mechanics for armour obliquity. Vehicles have different armour values in accordance to their facing, such as tank armour being thickest on the front, thinner on the sides, and thinnest on the back and on the roof. Mechanics for translating structures, fortifications, and buildings are written with the expectation that players and GMs will be able to either re-create historical structures in game by using open source intelligence and real-world hard factors to faithfully transcribe existant or even theorized defences into the game and play them out in the foreground, possibly using automation or macros to expedite the crunch.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Crunch</u></b><br /> <br /> <b>Dogs of War</b> and <b>Armoury</b> add very little crunch. <i>Dogs of War</i> includes a number of merits specific to military characters, and <i>Armoury</i>, <i>Armoury Reloaded</i> and especially <i>Hurt Locker</i> include a large number of merits for character options and fighting styles for personalizing your characters and NPCs and changing the way that they perform and play. Armoury introduces the concept of &quot;Force&quot; as a weapon characteristic for large weapons or explosives. This characteristic was brought back and expanded upon in <i>Hurt Locker.</i><br /> <br /> <b>Gods of Iron</b> greatly builds on and expands on the amount of options for weaponry, which includes mechanics for range brackets beyond the usual short, medium and long intervals, has expanded mechanics for combat, including new combat actions such as the tactical advance to move between cover and gain the benefits throughout, new melee additions, gunnery mechanics such as grazing fire, traversing and searching fire, as well as time-on-target, circular error probable and how kinetic rounds experience performance loss over distances due to air resistance, spalling, and how different shell types such as HEAT, HESH, and APFSDS rounds perform, as well as some historical rounds such as APCBC-HE and Common Shell. Explosives are set to be overhauled to follow guidelines according to rules derived from the Kingery-Bulmash engineering equations and fragmentation calculations set by the United Nations International Ammunition Technical Guidelines. A small amount of extra crunch is a consequence. Only a few merits topical to some of the advanced mechanics are included.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Supernatural Elements</u></b><br /> <br /> <b>Dogs of War</b> is first and foremost, a supernatural game at every step of the way and provides scope and suggestions for placing a supernatural twist on virtually every portion of the game. All of the influential figures are explicitly spelled out to be or influenced by the supernatural. <i>Dogs of War </i>includes a section specifically on the use of different supernatural character types in warfare and how they could fare.<br /> <br /> <b>Gods of Iron</b> is at this time with few exceptions largely agnostic of supernatural elements, leaving how the supernatural interacts with modern technologies as something to be determined from the consensus between players and GM. This is to help allow the players and GM to determine the genre of the game that they want to play. Very few weapons are spelled out with regard to how they impact the supernatural or other supernatural effects.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Air Operations</u></b><br /> <br /> <b>Armoury</b> has simplified rules for aircraft and missiles, which are treated the same way armoured fighting vehicles are: intended mostly as targets or environmental hazards for the contingency of modern military equipment meeting a large and/or powerful monster.<br /> <br /> <b>Angels and Aces</b>, which is currently planned, is intended to take into account facets of modern air combat such as missile no-escape zones, energy maneuvering, radar interception, and rates of climb or descent. A preview of some of the content planned for <i>Angels and Aces</i> is available, including a simplified version of the AIM-54C Mk. 60 Phoenix, which emulates time on target and several of the different modes that its seeker head is capable of operating in, including Track-While-Scan Active and Semi-Active Radar Homing, Pulse-Doppler Single Target Tracking, and ACM Active Radar Homing, each with their own special rules. <i>Angels and Aces</i> is currently in preliminary planning.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Naval Operations</u></b><br /> <br /> <b>Armoury</b> suggests treating a category of &quot;Very Large Vehicles&quot; as their own setting and location and simply uses them as framing for the action, suggesting that it would be pointless to track all the bombs, cannon fire, and missiles and to simply use the large vehicles to provide additional value to the focus on the player characters.<br /> <br /> <b>Seas of Steel</b> is also planned to follow either <i>Gods of Iron</i> or <i>Angels and Aces</i>. A preview of some of the content that could appear in <i>Seas of Steel</i> is available, with the inclusion of the US Navy 5&quot;/62 calibre Mark 45 Mod 4 Lightweight Naval Gun and the US Navy 16&quot;/50 calibre Mark 7 Naval Gun, which includes tables for the Mark 8 Armour-Piercing Capped shell, the Mark 13 High-Capacity shell, and the Mark 23 Nuclear shell. Current features include circular error probable mechanics as well as time on target, planned features currently not implemented include Angle of Fall to determine shell effect on both side and deck armour. The use of commercial off the shelf military simulators such as <i>Command: Modern Operations</i> to help make large scale military orchestrations is a planned feature for <i>Zero Hour</i>.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Guns and Other Weapons</u></b><br /> <br /> <b>Armoury</b> has a number of both ancient and modern firearms and ancient weapons in its table, with rules specific to each. In addition to providing several famous examples,<i>Armoury</i> also includes entries for &quot;Generics&quot; which also can be reskinned to comparable analogues of similar role, effect, and era, allowing the GM to be able to pick generic examples to arm their combatants if they aren't certain what to use, and then tailor them to be narratively appropriate.<br /> <br /> <b>Gods of Iron</b> has a number of iconic examples of some specific firearms but lacks generics. Ancient firearms in particular have detailed mechanics on the processes to load, fire, and ready them, breaking down the step of reloading a cap and ball musket from a matchlock and providing differentiation between the different combinations. Unlike <i>Armoury</i>, <i>Gods of Iron</i> doesn't have as much focus on melee weapons but includes a few specific historical examples.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Nuclear Weapons</u></b><br /> <br /> <i>Armoury</i> dedicates a paragraph to nuclear weapons, but except for a brief mention of &quot;assume everyone in ten kilometres is dead&quot; they are statless and mostly managed via GM fiat.<br /> <br /> <i>Gods of Iron</i> has simplified mechanics for nuclear weapon fireball radius, thermal radiation radius, fireball duration, ionizing radiation radius, and air blast radii for both total casualties and widespread destruction, which have been derived from real-world engineering calculations, as well as rules for deployment of Intercontinental Ballistic Missiles and Submarine Launched Ballistic Missiles via a special system called &quot;Playbook Time&quot; designed to emulate the fog of war, operational preplanning, and both launch on warning and survivable second strike capabilities such as road-mobile TELs and ballistic missile submarines. A number of nuclear weapons, including variable-yield nuclear warheads in service with the USAF and USN, and two reference examples: the LGM-30G Minuteman III and UGM-33A Trident II, are fully statted and able to be deployed via Playbook Time. This is done with the goal of capturing the goals, nuances and complexities of declarative nuclear doctrine, deterrance, and nuclear game theory. Nuclear fallout mechanics are currently in progress.<br /> <br /> <b><u>To close out...</u></b><br /> <br /> <i>Gods of Iron</i> was written assuming that the players and GM are going to be wanting to apply real-world tactics, techniques and procedures to their games at multiple levels, and attempts to capture these nuances in order to turn the game into a tactical simulation, so it doesn't add the same value that <i>Dogs of War</i> or <i>Armoury 1st Edition</i> does.<br /> <br /> I hope that I would be able to provide some value to other conventional Chronicles of Darkness players, although now that you mention it, that might be difficult to avoid treading ground that the other books already have.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My Project to convert Chronicles of Darkness to a data-driven <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd\" target=\"_blank\">Combined Arms Simulation: Gods of Iron</a><br /> My personal website, projects and games: <a href=\"https://puckerfactorgames.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://puckerfactorgames.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","name":"Jester-PFG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27707_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-03T11:26:42-07:00","id":1521993,"title":"","content":"I'm really curious about your rationale for nuclear weapons superseding every form of supernatural power in the setting for the purposes of killing anything and everything stone dead, and even going so far as saying they even ignore rules that would be contrary to this\u00b9. It's probably the largest outlier in these rules that otherwise, as you say, stay largely agnostic towards supernatural elements, and it's a striking one. Would a Mummy or Sin-Eater, whose whole deal is being immortal and reforming from nothing if they have to, be subject to this override that prevents reforming or reincarnating?<br /> <br /> This, along with the line that &quot;<i>No supernatural abilities may supercede, interfere with, or cause nuclear effects</i>&quot;, feels wrong for the setting, at least for me. One of the examples of what Archmages can do at Forces 6 is <i>&quot;Control of subatomic forces and nuclear decay by fusion or fission, both to cause elemental decay and to halt it. Creation of antimatter (particles which annihilate their equivalents, releasing energy).&quot;, </i>with this written up as the spell <i>Nuclear Cascade</i>\u00b2. There are Demons who, as Angels in service of the God-Machine, were regularly tasked with interacting with the stars themselves.<br /> \u200b<br /> The set of rules in this section seems to put nuclear weapons on a pedestal beyond what is warranted, casting them not as concussive force, heat, and radiation, but some sort of archetypal force of pure destruction that doesn't quite jive with the setting or with the rest of your otherwise grounded rules, in my view.<br /> <br /> <br /> 1. <i>Section 12.3: Nuclear Weapons in Game Terms</i> (specifically the last paragraph)<br /> 2. <i>Imperial Mysteries, </i>pg. 35","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/478-intrepid-vector","name":"Intrepid Vector","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar478_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"82"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-03T12:57:50-07:00","id":1522003,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Intrepid Vector</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd?p=1521993#post1521993\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm really curious about your rationale [truncated]</div> </div> </div> </div>Hey there, I appreciate the feedback and interest!<br /> <br /> No worries, I'm very happy to explain! I expected that section to be a tad bit controversial, and I should've probably put this in the PDF but I'll explain my intentions here as best I can.<br /> <br /> The reason for nuclear weapons being statted up as an archetypal force of pure destruction is simply for narrative purposes; the reason why was because what I wanted to do was to allow nuclear weapons to have a type of narrative consequence that would give everybody an extremely vested interest in making sure that they don't get used, or when or if my players finally decide to break the Nuclear Taboo, it will be both meaningful and have far-reaching consequences.<br /> <br /> The idea was to provide a means with which my players could expect to tie up a plot or an antagonist with absolute narrative finality and provide a sense of closure. In short, it's to close the book much like the way the bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki not only ended an an empire, but also brought about a massive change in culture and marked the end of an era, so that my players can open a new and potentially more interesting chapter in their chronicle, rather than just have that same antagonist come back and come back and come back again. Finally, the construction of nuclear weapons doesn't just represent the splitting or fusing of atoms, but represent the culmination of efforts of a major national economy to research and to produce; the placing them on this scale is to recognize this.<br /> <br /> The reasoning for &quot;no supernatural power&quot; is to prevent someone fresh out of character creation from using &quot;Elemental Fury&quot; from listing it as a tilt within 20 yards per dot of Wyrd, to avoid trivializing the narrative consequences of nuclear deployment as someone with Elemental Fury can cause an environmental tilt. It sounds like Mage has that exception explicitly spelled out for what they do already, unfortunately, I do not feel like any of the grounded stuff I do would square well with Mage.<br /> <br /> The bottom line on all of this is that it's a part of a gameplay element; it's to keep nuclear attack or nuclear warfare from becoming trivialized and make sure that when my players use Playbook Time, it has lasting, permanent, and absolutely setting-damaging consequences that echo across the entire world, and can't be simply trivialized away. We've very carefully tried to incorporate things such as recognizing both counterforce and countervalue capabilities and the value of deterrance (much like the French Force de Dissausion) along with things such as survivable second strike capabilities and launch-on-warning capabilities and make that a part of Playbook operations.<br /> <br /> Trivializing the nukes takes a lot of that narrative gravity out and what's otherwise a very planned and very deliberate exercise that my players would be sinking a great deal of effort into given the sheer amount of time and planning that they would be spending in Playbook operations to position assets, write their playbooks, and do the research, and it very much is not fair to them to just say <i>&quot;...and the big bad evil dude simply shrugs it off&quot;</i> when they could have spent a very long time putting in the work themselves and also made the deliberate choice to accept the literal and political fallout of breaking the nuclear taboo. It very much isn't fair to them for me to as a GM simply refute what could have been a lot of real-world research, my players going the extra mile to show their work, and a considerable amount of narrative escalation during the use of Playbook Time with a simple <i>&quot;but it says here on the book that this antagonist is immune&quot;</i> after they've exhausted all other conventional options and were willing to make such a sacrifice.<br /> <br /> In short, it's there to give nukes a bit of narrative gravity and keep their use both taboo, but also very meaningful while also giving them a distinct purpose in game that sets them apart, and present a very literal &quot;nuclear option&quot; for when the Godzilla Threshold is crossed.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My Project to convert Chronicles of Darkness to a data-driven <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd\" target=\"_blank\">Combined Arms Simulation: Gods of Iron</a><br /> My personal website, projects and games: <a href=\"https://puckerfactorgames.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://puckerfactorgames.com</a>","edited":{"userid":27707,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","user":"Jester-PFG","date":". 09-03-2023, 04:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","name":"Jester-PFG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27707_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-03T13:41:36-07:00","id":1522008,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jester-PFG</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd?p=1522003#post1522003\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The reasoning for &quot;no supernatural power&quot; is to prevent someone fresh out of character creation from using &quot;Elemental Fury&quot; from listing it as a tilt within 20 yards per dot of Wyrd, to avoid trivializing the narrative consequences of nuclear deployment as someone with Elemental Fury can cause an environmental tilt.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's\u2026 not what Tilts are. A Tilt is a stage hazard, and a nuclear explosion is <i>an explosion</i> \u2014 you could no more use Elemental Fury to directly nuke an area than you could use it to replicate a gunpowder explosion. Deviant and Promethean's mechanics for radiation are closest to the Extreme Environment rules, which are specifically contrasted against Tilts in being used for non-action scenes.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It sounds like Mage has that exception explicitly spelled out for what they do already, unfortunately, I do not feel like any of the grounded stuff I do would square well with Mage. </div> </div> </div>Jester, to become an archmaster you need to have the capacity for superhuman Traits, mastery of at least one Arcanum and near-mastery of three more, resolve a plot beat that basically can only be resolved with magic beyond the mortal limits of the Arcana, and succeed at a trial that annihilates you if you fail. Nuclear Cascade is an Imperial spell, which means casting it requires a Quintessence that can take an entire story to acquire for a singular use due to Quintessences needing to be things that are difficult to acquire with the resources of an archmaster.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The bottom line on all of this is that it's a part of a gameplay element; it's to keep nuclear attack or nuclear warfare from becoming trivialized and make sure that when my players use Playbook Time, it has lasting, permanent, and absolutely setting-damaging consequences that echo across the entire world, and can't be simply trivialized away. </div> </div> </div>I cannot find any reference to Playbook Time in the doc. Please clarify this?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> We've very carefully tried to incorporate things such as recognizing both counterforce and countervalue capabilities and the value of deterrance (much like the French Force de Dissausion) along with things such as survivable second strike capabilities and launch-on-warning capabilities and make that a part of Playbook operations.<br /> <br /> Trivializing the nukes takes a lot of that narrative gravity out and what's otherwise a very planned and very deliberate exercise that my players would be sinking a great deal of effort into given the sheer amount of time and planning that they would be spending in Playbook operations to position assets, write their playbooks, and do the research, and it very much is not fair to them to just say <i>&quot;...and the big bad evil dude simply shrugs it off&quot;</i> when they could have spent a very long time putting in the work themselves and also made the deliberate choice to accept the literal and political fallout of breaking the nuclear taboo.<br /> <br /> In short, it's there to give nukes a bit of narrative gravity and keep their use both taboo, but also very meaningful while also giving them a distinct purpose in game that sets them apart, and present a very literal &quot;nuclear option&quot; for when the Godzilla Threshold is crossed. </div> </div> </div>As with the issue presented in your Changeling thread, I get the distinct impression that an attempt is being made to make the system and setting do something it's not built for, because the closest Chronicles has come to having nuclear weapons be an issue of immediate concern is that one of the stories in Promethean: Strange Alchemies revolves in part around an old small-scale (Blast Area 150, deals 10 aggravated damage to all affected) nuclear bomb in Boston, and the potential outcomes of the switch being pressed include &quot;it's on a timer, allowing the suitcase nuke to be thrown into the water to halve its area of effect while you make a break for it&quot; and &quot;the bomb is old and just plain doesn't go off, much to the disappointment of the man hoping to use it to blow up an angry mob of Prometheans.&quot;<br /> <br /> Chronicles as a setting is tilted toward horror, mystery, and conspiracy moreso than direct strategic-level military combat as its primary focal action. Talking about avoiding reliance on the &quot;Rule of Cool&quot; is all well and good, but the fact of the matter is, as a sober glance at the healing times will tell you even if Hurt Locker et al didn't state it explicitly, this is a game whose sensibilities are tilted more toward the cinematic than the strictly realistic. More than that, despite it being quite far from the one-page-RPG side of the sliding scale of systems complexity, it's not a system whose mechanics are granular enough to make levels of damage above the teens worth tracking on anything smaller-scale than An Entire Building, which is why it tends to abstract giant monsters and large groups into stage hazards, cult/krewe/conspiracy actions, and Dogs of War's Strength/Firepower/Defense/Reserves/Strategic Assets systems or Deviant's &quot;goon combat&quot; mechanic. The types of stories the game is emulating are on the level of a Stephen King novel or a Netflix drama moreso than a wargame, and in those stakes a nuke is above the pay grade of almost everybody to consider as a standard sub-plot-device-level mechanic to be deployed trivially, which means if your players are bringing a nuke into play then it's coming on the timescale where the group of you have time to clarify the stakes of it being detonated.<br /> <br /> Like, not to yuck anyone's yum or anything, but when I look at a fan-supplement that's bringing triple-digit Durability scores into play and making a Tilt that's just there to model the presence of a nuclear explosion I can't help but feel we've leapt past the &quot;bucket of dice&quot; interpretation problem and into the realm of using chess pieces to play mahjong.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":111,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","user":"Satchel","date":". 09-03-2023, 04:44 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-04T11:10:08-07:00","id":1522060,"title":"","content":"Hi there!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd?p=1522008#post1522008\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I cannot find any reference to Playbook Time in the doc. Please clarify this?</div> </div> </div> </div>My apologies: I must have come up with the name &quot;Playbook Time&quot; after I'd written the mechanics for it, and only coined the moniker &quot;Playbook Time&quot; after I'd started implementing the groundwork for realistic communication with submerged submarines in the sequel, <i>Seas of Steel</i>. The mechanics for it however are currently in the book, and are fully playable; the process of strategic deployment is described after the Nuclear Weapons part, on Section 12.4 &quot;Strategic Warfare In Your Game&quot; at the time of writing, where it describes using nuclear weapons, and deployment of nuclear weapons using a series of written notes placed on the table. I'll briefly sum it up as well as my rationale for it below.<br /> <br /> Because strategic warfare has so much disconnect from the actual impact to the people making the decisions, I decided to take a special approach to it. In a nutshell, when players wish to use strategic warfare, I treat it by putting the game into a kind of &quot;Strategic Warfare Time&quot; meant to emulate the structures of nuclear contingencies, and the capabilities provided by early-warning radar (for example, the United States' Ballistic Missile Early Warning System and the newer Upgraded Early Warning Radar that's replacing the older systems), launch-on-warning and survivable second strike capabilities. This &quot;Strategic Warfare Time&quot; is similar to &quot;Initiative Time&quot; but in reverse.<br /> <br /> During this time I would be asking my players to place their all of assets (military facilities, nuclear launch facilities, cities, etc) on the map, then write down and submit the lists of targets they'll be attacking and place them face-down on the table. On our first playtest, I used to ask my players to specify which facilities they are sortieing from to which targets but that got too complex, so now I just ask them to tell me if they're using land based facilities or submarines. Once they're all in, we turn them face-up and then read off what sites, facilities, and cities were targeted and resolve them all one by one.<br /> <br /> It's easiest to use a Commercial Off The Shelf military simulator to help expedite the step of resolving the missiles. Although there's a game called ICBM which is essentially all this and much more, the one I've been moving toward using for doing orchestration of large forces in RPGs these days is <i>Command: Modern Operations</i>, which is a cost-reduced version of <i>Command: Professional Edition</i>. The consumer version sold to the public doesn't allow us to edit the unit database like the professional edition does so this means we can only use units that exist already in it, but for the most part that's mostly fine for what I tend to do. Because translating the results the simulator gives me into the game is often just as important as what it's trying to tell me, I'm including some basic tables for manually determining the results, including for circular error probable in the case that someone uses one or more of them in a tactical capacity where the accuracy becomes relevant within a certain number of metres. This process is repeated for each round of nuclear strikes until all the players have elected to &quot;pass&quot; when we turn their target lists over, or they've run out of weapons to exchange.<br /> <br /> At the present time, the use of these written notes meant to emulate an operations playbook (hence the name &quot;Playbook Time&quot;) is very simple and I haven't allowed my players to write any sort of cascading orders (ie: <i>&quot;<b>if</b> that happens, <b>then</b> do this&quot;</i>) to be created the way that a simulator like <i>Command: Modern Operations</i> does. However, that in some ways is something of a feature, to emulate the feeling of uncertainty and the fog of war. In real life, the time-on-target for an intercontinental ballistic missile can be as little as fifteen minutes, sometimes even less for a submarine launched ballistic missile if the submarine is willing to launch on a flattened trajectory at a coastal target, so as a result there's not that much time to make decisions on a case-by-case basis. From what I understand in the United States at least, nuclear warfare operations are done through the use of predetermined plans that are put into place and there's a complex system of designated survivorship and redundant communication systems put in place. Many analysts during the Cold War feared a decapitation strike aimed at taking out the leadership or command and control systems for the missiles and planned for it accordingly. The Soviet equivalent (which was inherited by the modern Russian Federation) was the &quot;\u041f\u0435\u0440\u0438\u043c\u0435\u0442\u0440&quot; or &quot;Perimeter&quot; system, which we sometimes called the &quot;Dead Hand&quot; as an equivalent which could autonomously launch the missiles in the event of a nuclear strike that decapitated the state. Great Britain has the &quot;Letters of Last Resort&quot;. This complicated system of using written notes and doing things play-by-play is meant to emulate all of this.<br /> <br /> In short, this system of players taking the time to declare where their assets are positioned around the map, build plans around those, then have those orders execute concurrently within the fifteen minute time-on-target by having everyone writing down their attack plans onto paper, and then having the GM open them all at the same time and have them take place is intended to make commanding strategic operations feel and play just like actual strategic operations.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd?p=1522008#post1522008\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's\u2026 not what Tilts are. A Tilt is a stage hazard, and a nuclear explosion is <i>an explosion</i> \u2014 you could no more use Elemental Fury to directly nuke an area than you could use it to replicate a gunpowder explosion. Deviant and Promethean's mechanics for radiation are closest to the Extreme Environment rules, which are specifically contrasted against Tilts in being used for non-action scenes.</div> </div> </div> </div>This makes sense. Because the fireball duration of some of the nuclear weapons can last for several seconds, I gave the fireball duration (to 10% of peak intensity) for each of the warheads and determined how many turns it would last for; some of the bigger examples are capable of lasting for several turns before the fireball reaches 10% of its peak intensity, and I wrote this as an area tilt to represent that. If you feel like it would be more fitting, I can get rid of the &quot;Nuclear Blast&quot; in the appendix and instead just write it to inflict all of the damage all at the same time within the fireball radius, which might save a bit of work.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd?p=1522008#post1522008\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Jester, to become an archmaster you need to have the capacity for superhuman Traits, mastery of at least one Arcanum and near-mastery of three more, resolve a plot beat that basically can only be resolved with magic beyond the mortal limits of the Arcana, and succeed at a trial that annihilates you if you fail. Nuclear Cascade is an Imperial spell, which means casting it requires a Quintessence that can take an entire story to acquire for a singular use due to Quintessences needing to be things that are difficult to acquire with the resources of an archmaster.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's good to know.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd?p=1522008#post1522008\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As with the issue presented in your Changeling thread, I get the distinct impression that an attempt is being made to make the system and setting do something it's not built for, because the closest Chronicles has come to having nuclear weapons be an issue of immediate concern is that one of the stories in Promethean: Strange Alchemies revolves in part around an old small-scale (Blast Area 150, deals 10 aggravated damage to all affected) nuclear bomb in Boston, and the potential outcomes of the switch being pressed include &quot;it's on a timer, allowing the suitcase nuke to be thrown into the water to halve its area of effect while you make a break for it&quot; and &quot;the bomb is old and just plain doesn't go off, much to the disappointment of the man hoping to use it to blow up an angry mob of Prometheans.&quot;<br /> <br /> Chronicles as a setting is tilted toward horror, mystery, and conspiracy moreso than direct strategic-level military combat as its primary focal action. Talking about avoiding reliance on the &quot;Rule of Cool&quot; is all well and good, but the fact of the matter is, as a sober glance at the healing times will tell you even if Hurt Locker et al didn't state it explicitly, this is a game whose sensibilities are tilted more toward the cinematic than the strictly realistic. More than that, despite it being quite far from the one-page-RPG side of the sliding scale of systems complexity, it's not a system whose mechanics are granular enough to make levels of damage above the teens worth tracking on anything smaller-scale than An Entire Building, which is why it tends to abstract giant monsters and large groups into stage hazards, cult/krewe/conspiracy actions, and Dogs of War's Strength/Firepower/Defense/Reserves/Strategic Assets systems or Deviant's &quot;goon combat&quot; mechanic. The types of stories the game is emulating are on the level of a Stephen King novel or a Netflix drama moreso than a wargame, and in those stakes a nuke is above the pay grade of almost everybody to consider as a standard sub-plot-device-level mechanic to be deployed trivially, which means if your players are bringing a nuke into play then it's coming on the timescale where the group of you have time to clarify the stakes of it being detonated.<br /> <br /> Like, not to yuck anyone's yum or anything, but when I look at a fan-supplement that's bringing triple-digit Durability scores into play and making a Tilt that's just there to model the presence of a nuclear explosion I can't help but feel we've leapt past the &quot;bucket of dice&quot; interpretation problem and into the realm of using chess pieces to play mahjong.</div> </div> </div> </div>I see what you mean a bit, I've had some difficulties adjusting to some of the system's and the setting's tacit assumptions, coming from a primarily realistic wargaming background. I'm actually quite grateful that people like yourself have taken at my stuff (because there is a <i>lot</i> of it, <i>much</i> more so than just nukes) and offer feedback on it.<br /> <br /> I understand that the genre shift is a big one from what I'm traditionally used to, but one of the advantages I feel a present-day setting confers is the capability for modern combined-arms combat at multiple levels, and I felt that the presence of <i>Armoury 1st Edition</i> and <i>Dogs of War</i> were a nod to the idea that we might be able to hold wars within Chronicles of Darkness. I felt like something was missing from them, so I've been working on adding some stuff where both of them left off. There's so much untapped potential in that arena that entire colleges are devoted to the study of warfare in the present day, and leaving the gameplay potential it offers out feels a little bit like missing out on a lot of the depth that a modern setting has to offer, especially when three books have come very close to what I've wanted.<br /> <br /> I've often held it that many Tabletop RPG systems can be very versatile, sometimes beyond what the original designers intended them to be for. The cover system and object Durability and Structure system was what I used for building the tanks. I had to determine armour values for them by using a bit of spreadsheet work and a few estimates, but more or less, that's how I treated tanks in this game. The rest of the stuff such as turret traversal speed, armour facings and armour obliquity, kinetic energy loss due to air resistance (DDO), the resolution and fineness of a vehicle's targeting system (Acc and TDD), and the different way the different shells behave are mostly stuff I've added in overtop, but at its heart, I tried to build on what was existing if I could. I generally use a heavy amount of automation to help me avoid the &quot;bucket of dice&quot; problem, usually with some extremely quick and dirty spreadsheet macros using stuff like &quot;=randbetween(&quot; and &quot;=vlookup(&quot; to try to help ease up on some of the complexity.<br /> <br /> My current group haven't had nukes enter play in our main game yet, but I'd also like to stress that the PDF is more than 260 pages at this moment in time, and only six or so of those 260 pages are dedicated to nuclear warfare. The rest is dedicated to conventional warfare, vehicles, and expanding upon combat, since those are things that my players can use more immediately and they're more appropriate to the situation. I find conventional warfare also to be a lot more fun (and less work) for me to play out than strategic warfare. The strategic warfare elements, while I do view them as having a large part in a national defence strategy and something that I think is something that I would like to work my players up to eventually at a future date, are still something I don't expect to bring into play except maybe near the end of a plot arc once something's really reached its boiling point and that kind of escalation is going to be both narratively and strategically appropriate. They're there if my players and I see the need to use them, and much like the use of aggravated damage to cap an escalation arc, would be there for an endgame for when the consequences merit such an escalation of force. So far, what where I've been focusing the bulk of my work has been in areas like armoured fighting vehicles, on the level of movies like <i>White Tiger</i> which has been mentioned a few times during this thread and in the PDF, but also there are some other movies that center around tanks or center around or include tank crews and follows them closely, like the movie <i>Kelly's Heroes</i>, <i>Battle of the Bulge</i>, <i>Fury</i> or <i>T-34</i>.<br /> <br /> I appreciate the feedback, and if strategic warfare is a dead end for now, I'll probably hold off on that and return my focus to advanced vehicle mechanics and other fireteam, squad, or platoon-level (4-person, 8-16-person, or 20-50-person) combat. The strategic warfare elements are something that might be nice to have, but even I don't use it that often.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My Project to convert Chronicles of Darkness to a data-driven <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd\" target=\"_blank\">Combined Arms Simulation: Gods of Iron</a><br /> My personal website, projects and games: <a href=\"https://puckerfactorgames.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://puckerfactorgames.com</a>","edited":{"userid":27707,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","user":"Jester-PFG","date":".  Clarification and grammar 09-04-2023, 02:35 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","name":"Jester-PFG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27707_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-04T11:55:59-07:00","id":1522062,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Intrepid Vector</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd?p=1521993#post1521993\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm really curious about your rationale for nuclear weapons [truncated]</div> </div> </div> </div>One alternative approach you've gotten me considering is to instead suggest that players consider the narrative implications and consider a sense of finality when deploying nuclear weapons, similarly to what I've outlined with my rationale above. This way, I can more closely level the theming with the supernatural-agnostic elements in the rest of the game, and leave each table with a bit more freedom to interpret that for themselves and apply those elements accordingly, using intentions as primarily a guideline, rather than try to state something authoritatively. Do you have any thoughts on that approach?\u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My Project to convert Chronicles of Darkness to a data-driven <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd\" target=\"_blank\">Combined Arms Simulation: Gods of Iron</a><br /> My personal website, projects and games: <a href=\"https://puckerfactorgames.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://puckerfactorgames.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","name":"Jester-PFG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27707_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-04T12:21:29-07:00","id":1522065,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jester-PFG</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd?p=1522003#post1522003\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The idea was to provide a means with which my players could expect to tie up a plot or an antagonist with absolute narrative finality and provide a sense of closure. In short, it's to close the book much like the way the bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki not only ended an an empire, but also brought about a massive change in culture and marked the end of an era, so that my players can open a new and potentially more interesting chapter in their chronicle, rather than just have that same antagonist come back and come back and come back again. Finally, the construction of nuclear weapons doesn't just represent the splitting or fusing of atoms, but represent the culmination of efforts of a major national economy to research and to produce; the placing them on this scale is to recognize this.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is exactly the <i>undue</i> reverence for nuclear weapons I was having trouble with. They're very big, very destructive bombs, but bombs none the less. I think elevating them to something almost holy, something uniquely capable of ending a conflict, is a dangerous way of thinking. <i>Why</i> do they have to be something that ensures narrative finality?<br /> <br /> From what I understand, the role of the bombs in ending WWII is still the subject of <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Debate_over_the_atomic_bombings_of_Hiroshima_and_Nagasaki#Opposition\" target=\"_blank\">much debate</a>, with a lot of military voices saying Japan was already in the process of surrendering and the bombings were militarily unnecessary.<br /> <br /> As for requiring the culmination of efforts of an entire nation, couldn't you say the same about, say, a fighter jet? An unmanned attack drone? Any other piece of cutting edge military equipment?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jester-PFG</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd?p=1522003#post1522003\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Trivializing the nukes takes a lot of that narrative gravity out and what's otherwise a very planned and very deliberate exercise that my players would be sinking a great deal of effort into given the sheer amount of time and planning that they would be spending in Playbook operations to position assets, write their playbooks, and do the research, and it very much is not fair to them to just say <i>&quot;...and the big bad evil dude simply shrugs it off&quot;</i> when they could have spent a very long time putting in the work themselves and also made the deliberate choice to accept the literal and political fallout of breaking the nuclear taboo. It very much isn't fair to them for me to as a GM simply refute what could have been a lot of real-world research, my players going the extra mile to show their work, and a considerable amount of narrative escalation during the use of Playbook Time with a simple <i>&quot;but it says here on the book that this antagonist is immune&quot;</i> after they've exhausted all other conventional options and were willing to make such a sacrifice.<br /> <br /> In short, it's there to give nukes a bit of narrative gravity and keep their use both taboo, but also very meaningful while also giving them a distinct purpose in game that sets them apart, and present a very literal &quot;nuclear option&quot; for when the Godzilla Threshold is crossed.</div> </div> </div> </div>I feel like the solution to this fear of a narrative anticlimax is much, much simpler than completely uprooting the setting's cosmology and overruling a vast swath of powers. If you, as the Storyteller, wish for your players to be able to use nuclear weapons to end a threat and have it be satisfying, simply <b><i>don't</i></b> introduce any threats that would be able to disregard being nuked to Kingdom Come. That is entirely within your power, as the final arbiter of what abilities and weaknesses the threats your players face have.<br /> <br /> Moreover, the Chronicles of Darkness is, largely, a setting for horror and occult mystery. If nukes are able to kill <i>anything</i>, this crowds out stories like\u2026<ul><li>The players have found the enemy's Bane. Can they convince someone in power to listen to them that a sprig of holly tied to an arrow will do much more to the monster than nukes <i>before </i>the bombs are in the air?</li> <li>The players nuke the monster and it doesn't do anything. Can they live with the regret of the collateral damage, knowing it was for nothing? Where do they go from here?</li> </ul>I see you've said you're tabling the strategic warfare for now, but these are some thoughts I've had rattling around.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":478,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/478-intrepid-vector","user":"Intrepid Vector","date":". 09-04-2023, 03:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/478-intrepid-vector","name":"Intrepid Vector","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar478_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"82"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-04T12:24:07-07:00","id":1522066,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jester-PFG</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd?p=1522062#post1522062\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> One alternative approach you've gotten me considering is to instead suggest that players consider the narrative implications and consider a sense of finality when deploying nuclear weapons, similarly to what I've outlined with my rationale above. This way, I can more closely level the theming with the supernatural-agnostic elements in the rest of the game, and leave each table with a bit more freedom to interpret that for themselves and apply those elements accordingly, using intentions as primarily a guideline, rather than try to state something authoritatively. Do you have any thoughts on that approach?\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>Sorry, just now saw this addendum after I left my big post. I think yeah, giving guidance on how nuclear weapons would impact a story instead of a hard and fast &quot;immune to magic and kill everything&quot; rule sounds like a much better direction.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":478,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/478-intrepid-vector","user":"Intrepid Vector","date":". 09-04-2023, 03:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/478-intrepid-vector","name":"Intrepid Vector","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar478_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"82"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-04T13:19:55-07:00","id":1522069,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Intrepid Vector</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd?p=1522066#post1522066\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Sorry, just now saw this addendum after I left my big post. I think yeah, giving guidance on how nuclear weapons would impact a story instead of a hard and fast &quot;immune to magic and kill everything&quot; rule sounds like a much better direction.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure, I'll go and get that set up and merge it for next update, which should be due with the Low Light/Night Fighting and Night Vision rules. I'm hoping to have that done hopefully this month!<br /> <br /> <b>Edited to Add: </b>I've just added something in interim, a brief draft to provide story guidance, and make the only hard rule &quot;this is for your group to decide&quot;. I've removed the &quot;Nuclear Blast&quot; tilt. I've filed this under a minor update, so it'll be separate from the Night Fighting and Night Vision Update. I've also clarified playbook time in Section 12.4.1, and added a small amount of fluff on what strategic warfare should mean in the game, to clarify what my intentions were when adding it, rather than just wisecrack &quot;because I can.&quot; This should go a bit of the ways to making it a little clearer on how and why it should be used.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My Project to convert Chronicles of Darkness to a data-driven <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd\" target=\"_blank\">Combined Arms Simulation: Gods of Iron</a><br /> My personal website, projects and games: <a href=\"https://puckerfactorgames.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://puckerfactorgames.com</a>","edited":{"userid":27707,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","user":"Jester-PFG","date":".  Issued the update now, to avoid needing to roll it into another. 09-04-2023, 09:35 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","name":"Jester-PFG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27707_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-15T14:24:54-07:00","id":1524117,"title":"","content":"It's about a month behind schedule, but the Low Light Engagements and Nightvision optical devices update is finally here. While I was at it, I also did a bit of reorganising of the document and I've started restructuring some of the source code used to typeset it, along with cleaning up the version control history to make it slightly easier for me to edit going forward. Some of the different vehicles and tank guns are now segregated by era, since it felt a tad bit awkward to have something like the old QF-6 Hundredweight six-pounder gun, a weapon from the 18th century, in the same section as the Rheinmetall L/55A1 120mm Smoothbore Gun, which is being rolled out to the newer Leopard 2A7Vs serving with the Bundeswehr or Armed Forces of the Federal Republic of Germany in the (at the time of writing) modern era.<br /> <br /> This new update adds mechanics for what's called &quot;Visibility States&quot; that add six different levels of relative visibility to the scene and objects within it ranging from daylight to total darkness. Rather than rely on an objective scale based on illumination, I instead chose to make it relative to the viewer to allow the GM and players to be able to quickly describe how visible something is to the given player or NPC given the equipment they have.<br /> <br /> The Visibility State works on a scale of 0 to 5 and has different examples of how visible something is base on ambient lighting conditions, along with detailed examples for each one. For instance, 5 indicates effectively unlimited visibility, and 0 indicates complete lack of visible light entirely, with 1 representing something like the conditions during a New Moon on a cloudy night underneath heavy tree cover in an old growth forest, and 4 representing something like the lighting conditions on a busy main street with plenty of street lamps, along with different and detailed spotting mechanics for each state.<br /> <br /> As a result, lower visibility states denote decreased visibility, which can not only mean darkness, but under certain situations, can mean increased amounts of bloom. Anybody who's played a night game of something like paintball or airsoft will know, that at least until autogated Generation III Nightvision Optical Devices that featured intelligent autogating arrived on the scene, one way to prevent people from looking into a structure from the outside at night while still being able to see outside without the use of NODs is to place a glowstick on the windowsill and let the bloom take care of this. I've tried to allow the different mechanics representing night vision devices to model this somewhat and take into account the different generations of nightvision optical devices through the eras, as well as the different pros and cons of using different configurations of nightvision optical devices, such as monocle and binocular configurations, as well as single tube biocular configurations that were popular back in the earlier days of night vision goggles and are still in use with some militaries that can't afford or source better, as well as milsim players and airsofters.<br /> <br /> All of these mechanics are intended to allow for approximate modelling of the different generations of night vision optical devices from the primitive Starlight Scopes of Vietnam Era and Korean War era fame as well as the Infrared Spotting Scope that evened the odds at the Battle of Iwo Jima, to the modern Harris L3 Panoramic Night Vision Goggles that sometimes pop up in popular culture, and were showcased in <i>Zero Dark Thirty</i>. I've even added some mechanics for stuff that is at this time somewhat experimental, such as the use of Colour Night Vision Technology, which is starting to be retrofitted to and work with White Phosphor Technology.<br /> <br /> At a future date I do intend to add some more gear, such as Infrared Target Pointers which also present laser illumination (for tracking of muzzle direction, for instance) as well as infrared illumination that works in the infrared to near infrared spectrum and provide illumination that's only visible to night vision optical devices that can amplify light in the Near Infrared Spectrum, however I wanted to get the NODs update out faster and felt that that wouldn't require all that much work to put together.<br /> <br /> In terms of gameplay impact, it's been my hope that the new additions brought by the low-light mechanics will indeed help considerably with offering many more tactical possibilities with handling low-light engagements and very much change the game entirely when it comes to fighting at night. We ran an earlier encounter with provisional and somewhat more simplified mechanics for lighting and shadows, and it had a significant input on how the different encounters were fought, and the way that the players needed to use lighting to their advantage. While it doesn't as of yet cover things such as lighting discipline, it added an extra dimension to the tactics, so I thought I'd try to formalise this a little more and make it something that brings this into the Chronicles of Darkness itself.<br /> <br /> Onto some of the other updates that have been included with this update:<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><b>Additions and Edits included with this Update</b></span><ul><li>I rewrote the introduction to make it a little bit shorter and more to the point, and more in line with the other game lines.</li> <li>The missing DDO values for the Rheinmetall 120mm L/44 and L/55 Tank Gun's kinetic energy penetrators such as the M829, M829A4, DM63, and DM73 APFSDS have been filled in. The lack of DDO values contributed to the rounds overperforming by as much as up to 7 AP.</li> <li>The missing DDO values for the Russian 2A46 and 2A46M-5 125mm Tank Gun have also been filled in, similarly to the above.</li> <li>The British Challenger 2 Main Battle Tank has been added, along with the L30A1 120mm Rifled Tank Gun with fully detailed tables, history, and mechanics. While the design itself is somewhat dated at this point and is being replaced by the Challenger 3, which is claimed to be a new take on the design, the Challenger 2 has detailed in-game stats and should be competitive on the modern battlefield with other modern main battle tanks including the Leopard 2A7V and the M1A2 Abrams SEPv4.</li> <li>Much like the tables for the Royal Ordnance 84mm QF-20 Quick-Firing 20 Pounder Tank Gun's Mk. 1 High Explosive Shot, the Royal Ordnance L7/US M68 105mm Tank Gun's M456A2 HEAT, M393A2 HESH, and the Royal Ordnance L30A1 120mm Tank Gun's L31A7 HESH round all use the Next-Generation explosive mechanics, which use figures derived from the Kingery-Bulmash Engineering Calculations.</li> <li>A section on the different mechanics inherent to different tank ammunition has been added, covering the rules for modern tank rounds such as APFSDS, HESH and HEAT, and historical tank round types such as APC/APCBC, APDS, APCR, APCNR.</li> <li>Tank Guns have been split into different categories, and are arranged by era.</li> <li>Tanks are now also split into different sections and are similarly arranged by era for easier navigation in the document.</li> <li>The rules for HESH rounds have been tweaked slightly in anticipation of the use of the Next-Generation explosive rules. HESH rounds now have their own AP characteristics for the pat formed by the deformation of the round on the target. Although the appearance of spaced or composite armour have blunted the advantages that HESH rounds offer in tank-on-tank combat (this is taken into account under existing vehicle mechanics), HESH rounds nonetheless offer considerable capabilities when it comes to demolishing structures; this is one of the reasons why until now, the British Army continued to hold out on using a rifled tank gun and with the focus being on the use of HESH rounds for their main battle tanks, even long after the rest of the world had switched to the use of smoothbore guns for tanks.</li> <li>Shotgun loads and damage tables have been tuned, after some research determined that the loads for the KS-23's 23x75mmR shells were loaded to significantly lower pressure than initially assumed. The shells are in fact, made from cardboard and the lethal loads are about on par with a 12 gauge shotgun, with the shotgun's true utility being in using less-than-lethal loads for riot suppression. I've added stats for 8 Gauge Black Powder loads, as well as suggestions for 8 Gauge Belted Industrial loads, which are used in boiler guns meant to dislodge clinker or carbon buildup from industrial kilns for comparison.</li> <li>The Benelli M4 Super 90/M1014 has had its tables and rules corrected from an earlier typo. Additionally, the Benelli M4 Super 90/M1014 Joint Service Shotgun has had its fluff and special rules expanded upon to allow for &quot;Ghost Loading&quot; (alternately referred to as &quot;Carrier Loading&quot;) which is described briefly in the United States Marine Corps Operator's Manual for the M1014 [See Footnote 1]. By allowing one round in the chamber, one round on the carrier, and seven rounds in the magazine tube, the M1014's magazine capacity effectively increases to 7+2. Ghost loading takes an extra turn.</li> <li>As an easter egg by request from one of the other members that I spoke to online, the Ford Model T has been added under Passenger Cars in the vehicles section, with fully detailed tables, history, fluff and special rules to make it uniquely feel like an old horseless carriage that dates back to the eras before the First World War. Now your characters too can party like it's 1912.</li> <li>Minor fixups and tuneups have been added and a few notes have been fixed and tuned.</li> <li>As we have discussed, nukes have been changed to encourage players and GMs to come to a consensus on the different narrative possibilities prior to gameplay begins.</li> </ul><br /> Next update will probably be the largest one so far and that will be implementing the Next Generation Explosive Rules for the entire game and all existing entries that haven't been moved onto Next Generation. However, I'm very happy that I got the NODs update down.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Further Proposed Additions - Tuning of Shotgun Damage Characteristics</b><br /> <br /> Some further discussion items I've wanted to table is a micro-update, specifically for shotguns. Earlier on, what I did was I added the &quot;Low Velocity&quot; rule for shotguns, which simply made them less effective against object Durability and especially against armour. However, recently, I've been considering with the &quot;Low Velocity&quot; rule and after some experimentation I've felt that this double-dips on the damage rating and sells them a tad bit short, especially given that a shotgun carries a Strength Requirement of 3, wheras a .30-06 Rifle carries a Damage of 4 and has a Strength Requirement of 2.<br /> <br /> Something to consider on this is that while a 147-grain .30-06 rifle with a muzzle velocity of 2,800 feet per second may have a muzzle energy of around 3,500 Joules, a 2-3/4&quot; 12 gauge shotgun slug weighs about 1-1/8 ounce, and will be leaving the barrel of a shotgun at around 1600 feet per second, for a total muzzle energy of 3,700 Joules. While a 12 gauge shotgun slug also won't penetrate an NIJ 0101.6 Class II Kevlar Vest, which a .30-06 Full Metal Jacket <i>will</i> at 50 metres, a 12 gauge buck hammer shotgun slug at the same range definitely has the potential to leave a much bigger hole in ballistics gelatine protected by one layer of denim fabric.<br /> <br /> Similarly, at around 25 yards, <i>five</i> shells of Federal Premium FliteControl 00 Buckshot, each of which contains nine pieces of 00 buckshot, fired offhand through either a Cylinder bore or Improved Modified choke will land all forty-five pieces of 00 buckshot within a pattern of around maybe 10 inches. 00 buckshot will usually penetrate around eighteen of ballistic gelatin reliably at that distance, which is more than the twelve inches of ballistic gelatine the FBI considers to be &quot;adequate.&quot; At the distance, a pattern of nine pellets of buckshot within an area less than 10 inches in diameter centered within the torso is enough to stand a good chance of landing at least one of the nine buckshot pellets in the heart and one or both lungs of an unprotected human and result in a first-shot fatality.<br /> <br /> Because of that I'm considering bumping the twelve gauge shotgun to &quot;4L 9-ag Low Velocity&quot;, rather than &quot;3L 9-ag&quot;, or where they are now at &quot;3L 9-ag Low Velocity&quot;. The Low Velocity rule in <i>Gods of Iron </i>doubles the effect of Object Durability unless the object is specifically listed as Fragile, and increases the effect of ballistic armour by +0/2. This in turn brings them up above parity against lightly armoured targets, but severely reduces their effectiveness against both Object Durability and any armour that has a ballistic rating. In Armoury 1st Edition, Shotguns also were Damage 4L 9-again, and so were full-sized cartridges such as the 7.62x51mm. This presents something of a compromise by bringing a 12 gauge shotgun up to be slightly above the level of a full-sized cartridge, but only at short ranged <i>and only</i> on unprotected, unarmoured targets. In essence this mechanically represents that a shotgun, in certain niche situations can be dramatically more effective than a rifle and produce the same muzzle energy, but only when those conditions are met; past about 40 metres, while the buckshot pellets can still retain lethal velocity, the accuracy becomes extremely questionable, even when firing high-quality, buffered loads with controlled flight wads.<br /> <br /> Finally, to counterbalance the shotgun spread effect, I'm considering halving shotgun damage by range interval; thus, a shotgun firing buckshot at short range would inflict full damage, then at medium range inflict half damage, then at long range a quarter of its original value.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Footnotes</b><br /> [1] U.S Marine Corps Technical Manual: Operator's Manual for Shotgun, Combat, 12 Gauge, Semi-Automatic M1014, 1005-01-472-3147, 30th November 2000, 18410698000. Refer to the operations section on loading the shotgun, Page 55.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My Project to convert Chronicles of Darkness to a data-driven <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1499154-homebrew-vehicular-and-combined-arms-combat-in-cofd\" target=\"_blank\">Combined Arms Simulation: Gods of Iron</a><br /> My personal website, projects and games: <a href=\"https://puckerfactorgames.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://puckerfactorgames.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27707-jester-pfg","name":"Jester-PFG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27707_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}